export const STORIES: readonly Story[] = [
  {
    id: "sweet-dreams-do-come-true",
    title: "Sweet Dreams Do Come True",
    teaser: "Helen Reynolds and Sweet Dreams bakery — hope that refuses to fade.",
    body: `Helen Reynolds, a kind-hearted, passionate woman in her mid-thirties, was the vibrant owner of 'Sweet Dreams', a local bakery nestled in the heart of a busy town. Renowned for her delectable pastries and heartwarming cupcakes, Helen was passionate about spreading smiles through her baked delights. However, despite her enthusiasm, life was a struggle for Helen, with her dream of expanding "Sweet Dreams" hanging by a thread due to a lack of financial resources. One sunny afternoon, after another busy day at the bakery, Helen noticed an email notification on her phone. It was from an organization named 'Dream Makers', a renowned group known for financially supporting small businesses. Intrigued, Helen opened the email. The question posed to her was simple: "If given funding, how would you expand and improve your business?" With an idea blossoming in her heart, Helen began to type out her response. The opportunity of a lifetime was in front of her, and Helen decided to pour her heart and soul into this chance, describing her dream of creating a community café space within her bakery for patrons to gather, converse, and connect over food. She imagined poetry evenings, vintage fairs, and local musician performances, fostering a connected and thriving community. She elaborated on her vision of sourcing ingredients from local farmers to support the community and use eco-friendly packaging. She hit send, her heart filled with hope. Days rolled into weeks, and the everyday hustle of the bakery consumed Helen's life again. The response she was eagerly waiting for seemed to have been lost, as there was no word from 'Dream Makers'. Yet Helen didn't allow her spirits to dampen. She continued to spread joy with her warm pastries, providing solace to countless individuals with every bite.On a freezing morning, almost a month after that life-changing email. Helen walked into her bakery to find a letter at the doorstep. It was from 'Dream Makers'. With bated breath, Helen opened the letter. "Congratulations, Helen!" "After reading your heartfelt response, we are happy to support “Sweet Dreams' with a donation to realize your vision." Helen was beyond words. Her eyes filled with tears as she smiled. The news traveled fast in the small town, and soon her bakery was filled with cheering patrons, celebrating together. Donations flowed in to support the initiatives Helen had planned:storytelling sessions for children, Sunday brunches for the elderly, and live music performances.Folks who'd never stepped foot in a bakery found themselves involved in a bustling local community, all thanks to Helen. Sweet Dreams turned not only into a bakery but a symbol of warmth and unity, a testament to the fact that dreams do indeed come true when one has the heart to strive for them, and there are generous souls ready to lend a hand. End`,
    playable: true,
  },
  {
    id: "magical-bakery",
    title: "The Magical Bakery",
    teaser: "Thomas and his little bakery in Pipersville — a sweet dream that came true.",
    body: `In a small corner of the world, nestled in Pipersville, a small town in Austria, stood a small bakery, adorning an old abode structure with its sweet, tantalizing aroma. The bakery was owned by a humble man named Thomas, a widower who single-handedly worked day and night to tend to his tiny business. He was renowned for his magical ability to create desserts that personified sweetness, affection, and warmth. It was a difficult time for Thomas, though. The recession had cut down on his regular customers, and, with no income source aside from this tiny bakery, his dreams of expanding his little world of sweetness faded each day. One bright, sunny afternoon, a stranger in a royal blue suit walked into the bakery. His eyes swept across the small bakery, lingering on the showcase of freshly baked treats. He ordered a single piece of Thomas's signature pastry, apple crumble. As he sank his teeth into the crumble, a smile transformed his austere face. Intrigued by the deliciousness, he lauded Thomas's talents and asked about the bakery's small size and minimalistic décor. Thomas, with a sigh, shared his aspirations of a bigger, majestic bakeshop peppered with more staff , diverse items, and higher footfall. The stranger seemed immersed in Thomas's tales and finally, after a long pause, introduced himself. He was Mr. Howard, a successful entrepreneur and philanthropist who had traveled the world helping local businesses reach their potential. "Well," Mr. Howard began thoughtfully, absently toying with his crumb-covered pastry fork. "I think everyone deserves a chance at achieving their dreams. And I certainly can't resist being part of a dream that involves delicious treats." With that, he vowed to donate a sizable sum to aid in the expansion of Thomas's bakery. Thomas was overwhelmed; his deep-rooted dreams, which had been on the cusp of fading into oblivion, suddenly saw the promise of blossoming into a beautiful reality. True to his word, Mr. Howard encouraged Thomas to utilize the donation wisely. He soon upgraded the bakery's infrastructure, hired capable, passionate helpers, and introduced a wider range of pastries. Word of the transformed bakery soon traversed through Pipersville, attracting food connoisseurs and loyal patrons from near and far. In no time, Sweet Dreams Bakery was a beloved name among dessert lovers. Each corner of the bakery, each mouthful of the delicacies served, embodied Thomas's passion for baking and his gratitude for the life-altering donation. Thus unfolded a tale where dreams, no matter how seemingly unattainable, do come true, sweetening lives along the way. Sustainability, kindness, and, above all, the soul-soothing power of delectable desserts found their sanctuary in this revived business, painting a vibrant picture of resilience and triumph. End`,
    playable: true,
  },
  {
    id: "believe-and-dreams",
    title: "Believe and Dreams Will Come True",
    teaser: "Young Erik in Denmark — belief that opens the way.",
    body: `In a quaint, tranquil village tucked away in the rolling green landscapes of Denmark, there was a fair-haired boy named Erik. Aged 10, stricken with an insatiable curiosity and filled with abounding vigor, Erik was known throughout the village for his vivid daydreams and strong belief in destiny. Erik spent most of his days at the village's edge, sitting on a rock by the glistening lake, lost in his dreams. His most profound dream was to become a world-renowned painter whose work could bring joy and inspiration to people far and wide. Erik would paint an imaginary world of vibrant colors in his mind, one where he believed his future awaited. His esoteric passion, however, was met with skepticism from the village. The majority were simple farmers and carpenters who saw Erik's dreams as nothing more than an idle boy's fantasies. They failed to understand the sheer power of imagination and belief. In this practical world, there was one person who shared Erik's fervor for dreams: the kind old village sage, Master Odgaard. He often told Erik, "Just believe, and dreams will come true." These words echoed within Erik's heart, fostering his dream with an unwavering conviction. With countless days spent practicing drawing with charcoal sticks and creating murals on the village walls, Erik's talent slowly began to shine. His world of art started inspiring and reaching the hearts of the villagers. But he remained unfulfilled, yearning to learn about the world of refined arts and colors. One day, Master Odgaard showed Erik a beautiful landscape painting, the work of an artist from Copenhagen. Erik was captivated by the exquisite detail and vibrant colors, yearning to create art like that. He expressed his ambition to Odgaard, who, seeing the burning desire in the boy's eyes, hatched a plan. Master Odgaard wrote a letter addressed to the prestigious Royal Danish Academy of Fine Arts, depicting Erik's artistic prowess and profound passion. He handed the letter to Erik, who traveled the long journey to Copenhagen, fueled by his dreams and Odgaard's encouraging words. Upon reaching the academy, Erik presented the letter to the dean and, asked to showcase his talent, created an impromptu mural on a white canvas. The dean was stunned by Erik's raw talent and imagination. Impressed, he granted Erik a full scholarship to pursue an education in the fine arts. The news of Erik's success reached the village, sweeping through the narrow cobblestoned lanes and cozy huts like a gust of wind. Everyone was inspired by the fulfillment of Erik's dream, and their belief in the power of ambition was revitalized. Years later, Erik returned to his village and became a renowned painter whose work was displayed in the finest galleries worldwide. His dream turned reality; he filled the village's air with hope and reassured Master Odgaard's wise words, "Just believe, and dreams will come true." In the end, a child's wild dreams transformed an entire village and brimmed it with belief, hope, and colorful imaginations. A rea ffi rmation echoed in Erik's life, whispering to every dreamer that dreams are not just figments of imagination but powerful propellants of passion capable of touching the sky if one genuinely believes. End`,
    playable: true,
  },
  {
    id: "dream-of-magic-of-music",
    title: "Dream Of The Magic Of Music",
    teaser: "Music that turns a quiet dream into living melody.",
    body: `In a tiny village nestled cozily between the vast mountains in Switzerland and sprawling plains, there existed a peculiar distinction. Each of its citizens was gifted with a dream—a profound vision of their destinies. However, the condition of this tantalizing promise was that they had to embark on a journey to decipher their hearts. This story revolves around a young boy named Eli, who is about to realize that beliefs and dreams stand meaningless unless you have the courage to seek help. Eli, a bright 12-year-old, has been fascinated by the magic of music since childhood. His dream was bestowed upon him on his twelfth birthday, as per the village's tradition. He received a vision in which he had become a famed harmonist, enchanting audiences worldwide with his melodies. Awed by the magnificence of destiny awaiting him, he decided to dedicate his life to learning and mastering music. His solitary journey of exploration had begun. Every morning, Eli would trek up the sturdy mountain, with the wind singing songs of the wilderness, leading him to a secluded spot. Here, he would practice creating tunes with his native instrument, a flute crafted out of an ancient pine tree. However, despite his relentless practice sessions, the rhythms he blew were arhythmic, often mirroring the chaos of a hailstorm rather than the sweet lullaby he yearned to create. His spirit was earnest, but his songs missed the allure. He was overshadowed by disappointment, but his belief in his dream didn't waver. One day, as he was caught up in his cacophonous exercise, a stranger passed by. She was Cara, a middle-aged woman known for her melodious voice across neighboring towns. Sensing his struggle, she halted and said, "Young lad, your faith in your dreams is strong, but your music seems deprived of harmony. Seek help, and let me assist you in unraveling the enchantment music holds." Eli, adhering to the village's teachings of self-realization, hesitated. "The magic of music should come from within me. "He cannot seek help." Weeks passed, and Eli's music showed no signs of improvement. Remembering Cara's words, he spiraled into contemplation. After wrestling with various thoughts, he realized that the journey of chasing dreams was not only about believing in them but also about learning, growing, and, most importantly, accepting help. With newfound courage, he approached Cara, his best friend, and swallowed his pride and said, "Cara, help me to create the music that resonates with my soul." Cara's wise eyes gleamed with delight and patience. Under her guidance, Eli learned the essence and rhythm of music. It was not just about blowing into the flute, but about understanding the language of silence and notes. He was taught to find harmony within himself before manifesting the same in his music. Eli realized the formidable power of asking for help. Cara's experience as a musician proved to be a beacon, guiding him away from disheartening stagnation towards remarkable growth. After months of tutoring, Eli played his flute atop his familiar mountain, emanating the tunes reflective of a bubbling brook, the rustling leaves, and even the harmonious echoes of the mountains. His dream stood before him as a magnificent reality. The villagers, attracted by his performances, were stupefied by the transformation in his music. His tunes encompassed an ethereal charm that bound and allured all those who heard them. He was no longer just another boy from the village. He became the young Harmonist, living his cherished dream. Eli's journey encapsulated the importance of seeking help when faced with adversity, despite one's staunch belief in their dreams. His story a ffi rmed that dreams could come true, not just with blind faith but with a little help from a friend and the courage to ask for it. Because, at the end of the day, dreams are not merely fantasies but patchworks of strength, belief, humility, and the wisdom to know when to seek a helping hand. End`,
    playable: true,
  },
  {
    id: "starlit-dream",
    title: "A Starlit Dream That Came To Past",
    teaser: "Under the stars, a dream finds its way into daylight.",
    body: `Once upon a 'not so' distant time in Norwegent, there was a young man named Ethan. Ethan was not much di ff erent from your usual teenager, equipped with an a ffi nity for video games, soccer, and a taste for adventure. However, he nurtured a dream that set him apart—a dream of starting his own business. He wasn't sure what kind of business it would be; all he knew was that he wanted to create something that would make people's lives better. After all, isn't that what a business ought to do? Every night, when the moon was high, the world was quiet, and the stars twinkled like a patchwork quilt in the inky sky, Ethan would drift away from his small-town reality and escape to his enchanted balcony. He'd lean on the rails, looking up at the stars, his hopes and dreams mirrored in their eternal shimmer. one for every challenge he'd overcome; one for each obstacle he'd face. His wishes, his dreams—they were all there, scattered across the vast expanse of the night sky. His routine was unvarying: finishing his evening meal, small talk with his parents, and then a beeline for his balcony. It was there that he found solace and drew his dreams from the celestial bodies above, nurturing them with his relentless spirit and quiet determination. When Ethan graduated from high school, he found himself at the crossroads of life, a fork formed by societal expectations and his dreams. He realized it was high time to put his dreams into action. But the path to achieving his business dream wasn't a mellow fairy tale, and our young dreamer knew it right away. He was prepared to battle dragons of hardship, slay ogres of doubt, and wade through swamps of naysayers. Ethan decided to major in business administration and took a part-time job at a local café. The books nurtured his mind's garden, adding to his arsenal of knowledge, while the café served as a first-hand experience of the highs and lows in a business's life. It was both chaotic and exhilarating. Even though he was just a barista, the business became his dojo, his practice ground. When he wasn't drowning in studies or brewing co ff ee, Ethan continued his timeless ritual of stargazing. The stars seemed brighter now, holding within them the infinite tales of man's triumph. Every failure, every victory, and every dream Ethan ever had seemed within reach. The co ff ee shop's aroma mingled with the scent of crisp night air, the perfect concoction for our stargazing dreamer. Fast forward five years, a string of sleepless nights, countless cups of co ff ee, a myriad of ideas sketched, countless dreams chased and brought down from the stars above, and Ethan was ready. It was time for him to make his dream a reality. To the world, he was still Ethan, with one di ff erence: a heart full of dreams and a bag full of experiences to match, from the warm smiles at the café to the tough lectures at the university. Driven by his dreams, Ethan started his enterprise as an eco-friendly goods shop—a balloon of dreams set afloat in the vast sky of reality. Every night, Ethan would still retreat to his balcony, gazing at the stars, his spirit conversing with them in their silent language. Each one held a dream he had realized; each one bore a dream for him to chase. They were no longer just luminous celestial bodies but cut-out tickets to his journey, mapped on the canvas of the cosmic realm. Ethan didn't just start a business; he left a legacy. He proved to himself and everyone who cared to see that dreams nurtured under the starlit night could indeed be realized under the sunlit day. His courage put wind in the sails of others, inspiring them to embrace their dreams, befriend the stars, and believe in something brighter, bigger, and better. In the end, Ethan found that dreams aren't just made as we sleep; they're given life in the wake of our constant endeavors and unfaltering faith. We all have dreams, each represented by a star in the infinite night sky. It's up to us to give life to these dreams, just like Ethan, under the watchful flicker of nocturnal lanterns chasing, realizing, and dreaming anew. End`,
    playable: true,
  },
  {
    id: "wish-upon-a-star",
    title: "A Wish Upon A Star",
    teaser: "A quiet Danish village, and a wish that changes everything.",
    body: `In a small forgotten village in Denmark, by the march of industrial progress, lived a young man named Ives. He was remarkable, not due to extraordinary physical strength or immense wealth, but because he harbored a dream. Every night, he would stare longingly at the starlit sky, articulating his dream of starting a business, keen to shine as bright as the stars overhead. "This Is His Story" Since childhood, Ives has always been fascinated by the concept of entrepreneurship. Whether it was selling lemonade in his backyard or crafting intricate wooden toys to sell at the local craft fair, Ives had always been passionate about creating something and sharing it with others. Each starry night represented a promise for a better tomorrow, another chance to make his dream come true. One day, an opportunity presented itself in the form of a map. The map was discovered in the attic of his ancestral home, depicting the locations of various herbs that carried medicinal values. The town's herbalist was already elderly and had no successors. Ives resolved to combine his innate knack for business with this newfound opportunity to start an herbal medicine shop. But starting a business was no easy task. Ives met with various obstacles: legal hurdles, lack of initial investments, finding the right location, and winning the trust of the townsfolk, who were traditionally averse to change. Yet his indomitable spirit was reflected in his eyes, which mirrored the stars he so loved. Each night, Ives took his worries and wishes and laid them before the stars, his sole constant and silent mentor. Over time, Ives managed to gather the necessary funds by selling some heirlooms and working extra shifts at the local smith. Then he sought the guidance of the aging herbalist, who, after testing Ives for several moons, shared the secrets of the herbs. The trust Ives managed to win from the elderly herbalist was paramount in his journey. Furthermore, he began to win approval from the skeptical townsfolk by offering effective cures for minor ailments. As the days turned into weeks and weeks into months, the pieces of Ives' dream slowly started to come together. The progress was as slow and painful as extracting precious metals from raw ores, but it was progress nonetheless. Every night, Ives would look at the stars, sharing his victories, setbacks, and lessons. They seemed to twinkle back at him as if understanding and encouraging him to keep going. In the end, after months of struggle and incessant perseverance, Ives' dream finally bore fruit. 'Ives' Herbal Elixir, the signboard read outside the newly minted shop, reflecting the sky, much like his eyes. The townsfolk, who initially showed skepticism, stood in awe of the young man who dared to dream and chase the stars.And every night, just as he had when his dream was merely an idea, Ives would look at the stars, sharing his gratitude, passion, and hopes for the future. Only now was he gazing not just at them but also at the reflection of his hard-earned success. Ives' story is an endearing tale of dreams, wishes, and dogged perseverance. It is a gentle reminder that the pursuit of any dream, like a journey through a star-studded night sky, demands patience and consistent e ff ort. But in the end, like Ives, we too can shine as bright as the stars if we dare to dream and strive to realize it. And as for Ives? He continued to dream, under the stars, of a young man with a never-ending supply of wishes. After all, the sky was full of stars, and so were his dreams. Ives, true to his promise, began learning about herbs from Harold. Each day he would visit Harold at dawn, learn about herbs until midday, and then run the shop until sunset. Easthaven's people were initially skeptical, but they gradually warmed up to Ives as the quality of his remedies remained consistent, thanks to his arduous training with Harold. His flair for business multiplied the shop's earnings, and soon he was able to reinvest the profits into growing fresh herbs, thus ensuring a steady supply of his remedies. Harold, though initially anxious, was content, seeing his beloved craft in good hands. In the end, Ives was able to successfully blend his love for business with the newfound duties he carried as an herbalist, to everyone's benefit. The townsfolk became more accepting of him, and Ives, previously known only for his shrewd business dealings, became an indispensable part of Easthaven—a novel role that surprised even him. End`,
    playable: true,
  },
  {
    id: "sweet-dreams-blossom",
    title: "Sweet Dreams Blossom Into Reality",
    teaser: "In Serendelle, dreams bloom into morning wonder.",
    body: `In the quaint and picturesque town of Arendelle, a little town in Norwegian, there existed an odd anomaly that made it stand out from the rest of the small, hidden settlements scattered across the realm. This unique and puzzling phenomenon affected every single inhabitant but didn't cause chaos or distress. Instead, it wove a magical tapestry of enchantment that blanketed the town like an invisible sheen. In Serendelle, dreams had a strange way of manifesting themselves in reality. Elders claimed the roots of this phenomenon could be traced back to an ancient fairy tale of a dream-woven damsel named Serene, who had blessed the land. Others said it was the result of an enchanted comet that passed over the town centuries ago. But regardless of its origin, one rule was as real as the cobblestones of the streets: when the inhabitants slept, their sweet dreams took form in reality. In Serendelle, nighttime was not just a period of rest. It was a time of magic, excitement, and anticipation for what the next day would bring. One such inhabitant, a young boy named Oliver, possessed an imagination as e ff ervescent as spring water, ebulliently bubbling with fantastical dreams. Every night, he would tuck himself into bed, anxiously counting the chimes of the town's clock tower. Each peal resounded with a promise of a new adventure awaiting him in his dreams and the following day. One evening, after the last chime, Oliver entered a bright, colorful dream where he was a brave astronaut, venturing into unknown galaxies. The following morning, to the delight of Serendelle's residents, silver stars littered the town square, a tangible reflection of Oliver's dreams from the previous night. On another occasion, his vivid dreamscapes conjured up a luscious, sweet-smelling Lolli-Tulip garden, a blend of candy and flowers. The following day, confectionary delights sprouted from the town garden alongside the regular blooms. In spite of the town's already extensive list of enchantments and attractive anomalies, however, it was Oliver and his fantastical dreams that stood as the pinnacle of attention and curiosity. Dreams were powerful in Serendelle; they took form, breathed life into the mundane, and painted reality with the vibrant colors of the subconscious mind. However, with great power comes great responsibility, and this was a lesson that the town of Serendelle learned one fateful night. Pestered by his snooping peers, Oliver's dreams turned tumultuous. His nightmares of a grumbling giant stalking the town sent a ripple of unsettling energy across Serendelle. In the town square, the cobblestones began to tower and morph into a gargantuan figure. Panic swept through the streets, a chilly, frightful reminder of the chaos that uncontrolled reams could sow. However, just as hope seemed to desert the townsfolk, a plan was devised. Oliver was gently awakened, the terror of his dreams evaporating from his consciousness. As he opened his eyes, the massive figure began to crumble, returning to the harmless pavements from whence it came. This event served as a wake-up call. Serendelle's inhabitants learned a valuable lesson in the importance of peace and harmony. They came to understand the pivotal role that not only dreams but also emotions played in their lives. Oliver, strong, resilient, and empowered, promised to maintain a firm grip on his emotions and to never let uncontrollable fears govern his dreams. He aspired to harness this unique power and use it to bring nothing but joy, wonder, and fascination to the world around him. From then on, the town of Serendelle lived in harmony, embracing its exceptional circumstances. They rejoiced in their ability to convert dreams into reality and found solace in the subtle whispers of the night, eagerly anticipating the wonders of the following day. And so, in the heart of Serendelle, night after blissful night, the mystical power of dreams continued to blossom, watered by the collective calm and unity of its people. Through their deep slumber, the townsfolk continued to blur the line between reality and the surreal, between the conscious and the unconscious, crafting a world where sweet dreams truly did blossom into reality. End`,
    playable: true,
  },
  {
    id: "dreams-dipped-in-sugar",
    title: "Dreams Dipped in Sugar",
    teaser: "Gelosia in Italy — sweetness, courage, and dreams that rise.",
    body: `Way back in time, in the charming town of Gelosia in Italy, renowned for its forested mountains and chocolaty rivers, lived a little boy named Theo. He was an ordinary boy who dreamed extraordinary dream dreams that tasted like sugar and smelled of vanilla essence. Dreams that were sprinkled with cinnamon stars and drizzled with honey sunbeams These dreams were far from ordinary, for to dream them required a blend of innocence and audacity that only little Theo possessed. Every night, Theo would rest his head on his pillow, close his eyes, and voyage forth into his confectionery kingdom. Ripe cotton candy trees, rivers made of liquid chocolate, sweet lemonade rain, and coconut snow it was a kingdom where fantasies thrived. Amid the sugar-dusted hills and marmalade skies, Theo dreamt of creating the grandest candy castle ever tasted by man or dreamed by a child. He aimed to craft this castle out of jelly beans, marshmallows, and drizzled caramel, all constructed on honeycomb foundations. Days turned into weeks and weeks into months, yet this dream eluded Theo. He could taste the sweetness of the victorious moment in his slumber, but the vision of the candy castle was always fuzzy; the construct was gossamer, like a mirage dancing on the horizon that dissipated upon approach. His heart was firm and resolved unwaveringly, but constructing the castle from sugar and dreams seemed like an elusive reality. One day, Theo decided to visit the town's wise old lady, known as "The Dream Weaver," who resided atop the Frosted Mountain. She wasn't just gifted with the wisdom of age; she held the mystic powers to read, interpret, decode, and often guide the dreams of the people of Gelosia. With determination in his eyes, Theo embarked on the journey, fueled by his confectionery aspirations. When he reached her, he dropped a courtesy call to the old woman. He narrated his dream, illustrating the candy castle he desired with such fervor that it made the ends of the old woman's mouth twitch upwards in an almost smile. She studied the boy through her deep-set eyes for a moment and then spoke: "Ah, little Theo, dreams that are dipped in sugar often attract fantasies rather than realities. But to make them tangible, remember, "Patience is the sweet syrup that binds dreams, and hard work is the heat that sets them." She then proffered him the sachet of precious dream sugar she was famous for. The words of wisdom stuck with Theo. He pondered it all the way down, the comforting weight of the dream-sugar sachet fostering trust in his heart. That night, he dreamt again, but this time with patience acting as his syrup and intense dedication serving as the heat. He then slowly began to feel the candy castle take shape, its dreamy landmark becoming less a dream and more a reality with every passing night. Seasons changed. The cotton candy trees lost their fluff , only to regain it again. The liquid chocolate rivers froze and melted, yet Theo was consistent. Every night, he would return to his confectionery kingdom. With each passing dream, his candy castle took on more solid form—the honeycomb foundations became firm, the caramel didn't trickle, and the jelly beans turned vibrant. A few years later, while the world slept, a dream was manifested. Theo's recurring dream had taken life. The Candy Castle, a masterpiece of will, hard work, dedication, and patience, stood mighty and sweet against the marmalade sunrise, a testament to all dreams dipped in sugar, waiting to be realized with the right ingredients. Theo woke up that morning, a sweet taste lingering on his lips. He knew without inspecting his confectionery kingdom that his dream castle now stood in all its might. His dreams dipped in sugar had finally taken a form, a sweet reality of dream fulfillment. His joy was immense, but his satisfaction was more immense. That day, atop his Frosted Mountain, the Dream Weaver smiled. For she knew, a dream, no matter how sugary, had found its confectionery castle. And down in the town of Gelosia, a little boy who once embarked on a dream quest was now a Dream Master, his sweet castle proof that all dreams dipped in sugar, could indeed, manifest into a delightfully sweet reality. End`,
    playable: true,
  },
  {
    id: "journey-blissful",
    title: "A Journey into the World of Blissful Realities",
    teaser: "A gentle walk into a world where calm dreams feel near.",
    body: `In a quaint old town named Serenity's Grace lived our idealistic inventor, Dr. Ciliv Romulus. In the heart of his secluded mansion, he invented a spectacular machine where you can enter your dream world; he calls it the 'Dream Escapade'—a device capable of exploring and manipulating dreams. Dr. Romulus, a fervent believer in the power of dreams, viewed this machine as an escape for those who yearned for supreme happiness but were shackled by reality. However, as the people of Serenity's Grace discovered, the line separating soothing dreams from stark reality was much more delicate than they imagined. After tireless years of research, the 'Dream Escapade' was ready for its first run. The machine, a complex network of wires and shimmering holographic displays, was unlike anything the townsfolk had ever seen. Though apprehensive, they agreed to participate in what Dr. Romulus christened the dawn of a new era of happiness. The first group of townsmen embarked on their dream-filled journey, their minds delicately entwined with the whimsical machine. Dr. Romulus watched with teetering anticipation as each expression transformed from sheer skepticism to delightful astonishment. Every participant recounted extraordinary tales of dreams so vivid and lifelike that they were almost indistinguishable from reality. Emboldened by the machine's early success, more townsfolk began to indulge in their innermost desires and dreams, seemingly detaching from reality's grim hold. The moment our apprehensive baker Jonah revealed his dream of becoming the world's greatest lute player and woke up to a refined mastery over the instrument, the entire town burst into collective gasps. Sweet dreams began twisting the fabric of reality in Serenity's Grace. Amy, the librarian, relished her dream of owning an exotic menagerie, only to wake up surrounded by chirpy cockatoos and bashful pangolins in her humble abode. Word of these astonishing occurrences spread like wildfire, turning the tranquil town into a whimsical dreamscape. However, as dreams started creeping into the waking world, people began losing grip on reality. The town was teetering on the brink of chaotic euphoria and confusion. Days were filled with bakers strumming lutes, librarians chasing wildlife, and farmers flying with newfound wings—drastically diverse dreams blurred the lines of actuality. Disturbed by the unexpected turn of events, Dr. Romulus desperately tried to find a solution. His intention had been to provide solace through dreams, not to birth a reality infused with uncontrolled fantasy. The border between dreams and reality had been gambled away, plunging Serenity's Grace into a fantastical twilight where the real seemed unimaginable and dreams were reality's candid veil. After ceaseless work, Dr. Romulus found his salvation in a dream paradox. To restore balance and separate the two worlds, he had to venture into the town's collective dreamscape and introduce a dream so outlandish, so impossibly contradictory, that the dreams would recoil back into the realm of sleep, unable to sustain themselves in reality. Dr. Romulus entrusted himself to the "Dream Escapade." He dreamed of the simple serenity's grace, untouched by the machine's influence. In his dream, the 'Dream Escapade' did not exist, and neither did its reality-twisting effects. The paradox that a machine, whose entire premise was to make dreams real, did not exist, unleashed a shockwave through the dream realm. As the inventor awoke, the sweet dreams kissed reality goodbye and retreated into their slumbering realm. The baker found himself devoid of any musical prowess, while Amy's exotic pets vanished into thin air. The chaos subsided, and normalcy was restored in Serenity's Grace. However, the impact of the encounter remained. The town was left with a profound understanding of the intricate dance between dreams and reality. Understanding that dreams are soft whispers of the mind, best left unraveling at night, and reality is a blend of joys and trials best lived awake Dr. Romulus dismantled his machine, letting dreams remain in their home, spun by reverie and rested by sunrise. In this gentle journey into the world of blissful realities, the people of Serenity's Grace learned an important lesson: sweet dreams indeed do come true, but not all dreams are meant to be lived in the waking world. After all, what fun is a dream if it loses its mystery and blends into everyday reality? End`,
    playable: true,
  },
  {
    id: "unwrapping-gift",
    title: "Unwrapping the Gift of Dreamy Realities",
    teaser: "Opening the quiet gift hidden inside our dreams.",
    body: `In a desolate reality, there was a teenager named Ethan. His world was shrouded in layers of melancholy and sorrow. Surrounded by the noise of turmoil and discontent, the bitterness of his troubled reality was unrelenting. It was as if he were shackled to an endless abyss of relentless worries and heavy disappointments. However, when Ethan slept, he found his only solace: the land of sweet dreams. Sweet Dreams is an idyllic realm filled with the warm pastels of happy thoughts and streets paved with the pure gold of hearts' desires. In this serene dreamscape, Ethan became a majestic phoenix, his radiant wings fanning the air as he soared freely through the skies of ecstasy. Here, in the sanctuary of his dreams, Ethan found peace, freedom, and an illuminating splendor that lightened the burden of his sombre reality. One exceptional night, as Ethan drifted into sleep, a silver light surrounded his bed, a gentle force lifting him as his eyes fluttered shut. As his consciousness began to blur, there was a whisper, soft yet clear. "Hold on tight, dear Ethan. "You are now about to unwrap the gift of dreamy realities. Suddenly, the clouds of the dream realm fluttered away, and Ethan stood in an open clearing of iridescent flowers whose scents held the promise of mystical adventures. His surreal surroundings bore an odd sensation, like a memory engraved deep within. Unbeknownst to him, his dreams had now become his reality. In the opening days of this new existence, Ethan is revealed in the inexplicable yet alluring landscapes, steeped in harmonious colors and imbued with an unshared tranquility. It was like his dream reality had begun to conspire against him, drastically pulling him away from his sweet solitude. Amid the beautiful madness, Ethan realized that the land of sweet dreams was no longer a simple escape from his troubled reality but rather a living, breathing entity keen on He swam in rivers of liquid sunlight, danced with phosphorescent butterflies, and feasted on fruits of euphoria. It was an existence devoid of pain, bursting with delight—a paradise carefully knitted from the gemstones of imagination. As days turned into weeks and weeks blurred into months, Ethan experienced a novel form of liberation. He was no longer a mere visitor in the land of sweet dreams; he was its inhabitant, erasing the last vestiges of his troubled reality. Nevertheless, dreams are not always made of sugar and spice. The wonderland of dreams soon began to demonstrate an uncanny capability to shift from sweet to sour. There was growing unrest in Ethan's heart as pleasant dreams unraveled into life-altering challenges. The lovingly colored skies turned into daunting storms. The rivers of liquid sunlight started to scorch. The phosphorescent butterflies began to sting. Each breathtaking vista began to morph into a chilling facet of his worst fears and insecurities, forcing him to relive his realities, only this time with more intensity and added surrealism. shaping him through tough love. He was not merely surviving in the realm of dreams anymore. He was now living it, feeling each prick and praise, each setback and success, each ache and applause. Over the course of time, Ethan noticed his inner phoenix maturing, growing brighter and stronger with every storm it faced. He understood how his sweet dreams' transformation into haunting realities was not torment but a gift wrapped in complex layers of growth, resilience, and understanding. Staring at his reflection in the once-burning river, now a source of sterilizing knowledge, Ethan saw how much he had changed. His eyes held wisdom, his heart echoed bravery, and his spirit radiated resilience. The initially terrifying shift from his troubled reality to a challenging, dreamy reality had become the greatest teacher he never knew he needed The land of sweet dreams, once his ideal escape, had turned into a transformative journey that became the turning wheel for his evolution. It was no longer about escaping reality but about unwrapping the enigmatic gift of dreamy realities. In this elusive, nurture-driven land, Ethan didn't just discover an alternative reality but stumbled upon a mirror reflecting the best version of himself. In living his dreams, he had grown, matured, and transformed into a phoenix who not only dreamed of a better life but had the courage and wisdom to shape and embrace it. Thus, Ethan, once a simple teenager escaping his troubled reality, was now a phoenix, reborn from the ashes of his dreams, ready to conquer the grand canvas of life, awaiting his splash of colors. With his heart full of lessons and character molded by the land of Sweet Dreams, he could now claim, "In my dreams, I did not just find an escape. I found myself." In the end, Ethan was whisked away to wonder, and he successfully unraveled the gift of dreamy realities. Here unfolded the saga of Ethan and his delicious dalliance, with the tantalizing turmoil of dreams morphing into realities—a transformational taste of joy, fear, and uncharted discovery that shaped his odyssey and, ultimately, the essence of his existence. End`,
    playable: true,
  },
  {
    id: "delightful-dreams-weaver",
    title: "A Tale Of Delightful Dreams Weaver",
    teaser: "The weaver who stitches hope into night-time visions.",
    body: `Once on a tempest-tossed night, shrouded in hushed whispers of the wind and the melancholy lullaby of festival bells in the distance, there lived a young maiden named Aria. Aria lived in an antiquated hamlet, often forgotten by cartographers, called Capella, a small village in Italy. She was a dream weaver who wove not silk or wool but dreams, filling the village with enchanting hues of wonder, delight, and possibilities. Yet she was always lost, frolicking in the world of sweet dreams that emerged from her innocent heart. The villagers oftentimes noticed Aria perched on her window sill, her sparkling azure eyes lost in the infinite cosmos dusting the sky. Aria was indeed unique; she embraced the impossible and took great joy in traversing the land of delightful dreams. But it wasn't all dreams and no action. Aria found a way to convert her dreams into reality, and that journey started with a magical stone named "Seraphim's Tear." She discovered in the abandoned well at the heart of Capella that the stone was said to have divine properties and was believed to bring one's dream into existence. But only if the dreamer is pure at heart and their dream is benevolent. As days rolled into nights and seasons made way for seasons, Aria nurtured an audacious dream. With Seraphim's tears by her side, she yearned to transform Capella into the Dream Haven, a sanctuary where dreams would come alive and humans could find solace in their aspirations. However, to make it possible, she had to reveal the secret of Seraphim's tear to the village elder, Caelum. A stern yet fair man, Caelum was skeptical of the stone's authenticity. "My dear, I applaud your unwavering faith in this mythical stone. But remember, 'It is our will, and not a stone, that can bring dreams into existence." Caelum said, dismissing the idea. Aria was crestfallen, but she didn't lose hope. She held onto her belief, her eyes a pool of resolute determination. Undeterred, she decided to prove the old man wrong and show everyone that the dreams woven in the realm of delightful dreams could indeed come true. Braving through the murals of doubt, ridicule, and skepticism, Aria ventured forth, her heart throbbing with her dream. She gathered the villagers, ushering them into the world of dreams. She urged the villagers to express their deepest desires and let them be heard throughout Capella. Aria then asked them to touch Seraphim's tear, and they did, one after another. Weeks rolled into months as every night, the villagers would gather around to share their dreams and hopes. Against the backdrop of twinkling stars, Aria wove their dreams, crystallizing the imaginations that once resided in the heart into tangible hopes. Miraculously, the hamlet of Capella began changing. The ripples of change that started at that gathering site gradually caused a massive transformation. The once antiquated and forgotten Capella flourished, reflecting the dreams and desires of its dwellers. The people, skeptical and anxious at first, now had a glow in their eyes—a flame of wonder and anticipation. Their once monotone daily life colors turned into vibrant hues and shades, distinctly cheerful and lively. Laughter, joy, and hopes echoed in each corner of the flourishing hamlet. Caelum, caught in the spectacle, could not hide his amazement. With a tear-stained face, he confessed, "Sometimes, the impossible isn't really the impossible; we just hadn't thought about embracing it with the right attitude." "You have taught us an invaluable lesson, Aria." The once-lost Aria, now found, is saddened yet proud to have turned her delightful dreams into reality. Realizing once more that dreams do come true, Aria kept fostering her belief, continuing her joyful walk into the realm of delightful dreams, inspiring every soul who desired and strived to embrace the impossible. And so went on the celestial ballad of Aria, the dream weaver of Capella, emboldening every heart to believe in the magic of dreams and the strength to conquer the impossible. End`,
    playable: true,
  },
  {
    id: "sweet-dreams-arent-fantasy",
    title: "Sweet Dreams Aren't Just Fantasy",
    teaser: "Proof that sweet dreams can be more than make-believe.",
    body: `Once upon a time, in the quaint little town of Dreamville, a place in Raleigh, North Carolina, lived a young girl named Emily. Eleven-year-old Emily was an e ff ervescent girl who held a universe of dreams and a kingdom of imagination in her eyes. She had an endearing charm about her that radiated hope, a hope that unveiled infinite possibilities. She was residing in the quaint vicinity of reality but was ceaselessly seduced by the tantalizing allure of dreams. One silent night, under the velvet darkness with a silver sprinkle of stars, she crept under her lavender blankets and drifted o ff to sleep. What she didn't realize was that this was a night that would unlock a journey into a realm of dreams, where her sweetest dreams would translate into realities. Awakening in the world she constructed from her fantasies, Emily found herself in 'Bliss Ville'—a land of candyfloss clouds, chocolate streams, and houses made of sweets. The trees were laden with candies, laughter echoed in the air, and the skies painted a canvas of rainbows. Flowing through Blissville was the sweet symphony of happiness, the scent of enchantments, and the flavor of the rarest joys. Bliss Ville was a true manifestation of Emily's innocent dreams. It was here that she met Mr. Serendipity, the mayor of this land. He was a charming elderly man with twinkling eyes that held the spectacles of experience and a shiny pearl-white beard dangling down to his chest. The kind mayor guided Emily around Bliss Ville, where she enjoyed the sweet realities of her dreams. They sang with the musical murmur of the pink brooks, danced with the caramelized sun rays, and interacted with the friendly cotton candy clouds. This world was no less than a living dream, a landscape dipped in the colors of blissful realities. However, it was more than pure joy in Bliss Ville; Emily found that every corner of this dreamland had something to teach her. She realized that bliss wasn't just confined to a sugar- coated reality but was expanded into life's most profound virtues like kindness, hope, courage, and empathy. In the heart of Bliss Ville stood a mighty candy cane tower, the Abode of Dreams. A wise old sage named Solomon resided there. He unraveled some mysterious enigmas and virtues of life for Emily. He shared, "Bliss Ville represents your dreams, and living them isn't confi.ned to this world only. "For dreams aren't just slumbers but are indeed the whispers of your soul." Emily rushed to Sage Solomon, her heart pounding. He listened to her dream and explained, "Dreams are not just illusions, my child. They are a reflection of your pure heart and great ambitions. This dream is a sublime reality that Blissville owes its existence to, much more than just candies and chocolates." Stunned, Emily absorbed the revelation that Bliss Ville was not just about the candies and chocolates but beyond those sweet delights to the dreams, hopes, and aspirations of its people. She, a mere dream-weaver, held Bliss Ville in her heart! Emily, henceforth, understood that dreams didn't merely paint pictures of her subconscious but encapsulated the essence of Blissville. She devoted herself to nurturing her dreams, which breathed life into Bliss Ville. With newfound wisdom, she whispered into the silent night, "Bliss Ville is not just a land of candies and chocolates; it's the land of our dreams!" Thus, Emily became the heart of Bliss Ville, fueling it with dreams and hopes while cherishing the teachings of Sage Solomon deep within her. And so, Bliss Ville thrived, not only on candies and chocolates but also on the sweet dreams borne from pure hearts and hopeful minds. After spending a day in Bliss Ville, Emily went to sleep with a satisfying smile, only to wake up in her small room back in Dreamville. Realizing it was a dream, she couldn't help but feel a pang of disappointment. However, the memories of Bliss Ville were vivid and real, reflecting the impact they made on her. The days didn't remain the same for young Emily; she blossomed like a colorful flower with new enthusiasm and motivation. She began to dream more beautifully, work more sincerely, and live fully, carrying the essence of Bliss Ville in her heart. She realized that, much like her journey to Blissville, dreams could be manifested into reality through the sheer power of hope, determination, and a sprinkle of magic. And so, Emily continued to dream and breathe life into them, proving that sweet dreams aren't indeed just fantasies; they could be a delightful reality too. From that day forward, Emily not only dreamt but lived with hope, courage, and motivation. After all, for her, her dreams were not just slumbers but indeed a delightful dive into the landscape full of blissful realities. End`,
    playable: true,
  },
  {
    id: "mystical-labyrinth",
    title: "The Mystical Labyrinth Of Dreams And Reality",
    teaser: "A labyrinth where dreams and reality softly meet.",
    body: `Once upon a dreary existence in humanity's dystopian future, the concept of dreams was outlawed. A sombre pall hung over the world, where nocturnal whimsies and waking fantasies were regarded as high treason. In the heart of the desolate city lived Leo, a struggling artist whose only true solace was delving deep into his dormant imagination. His masterpieces often flickered with elements of advanced whimsy sprinkled on a canvas of grim reality. One fateful evening, Leo was visited by an enigmatic figure who introduced himself as Morpheus, an emissary from an underground secret society named the Oraculum. They were the last vestiges of rebellion, a clandestine consortium of dreamers. Their secret was powerful—their dreams could transcend mere thought and manifest into reality. Lured by the tantalizing prospect of such freedom, Leo joined their ranks. The Oraculum's headquarters was an uncanny place, filled with a cornucopia of colors, surreal landscapes, and exquisitely bizarre creatures. It was a living, breathing painting sculpted from the collective dreams of its members. Leo's training began under the wise Morpheus. He was taught to weave dream threads into tangible strands of reality. What astounded him was the manifestation of his dreams, as fantastical landscapes appeared before his eyes. His art became a conduit and a critical tool for the revolution that the Oraculum planned against the hostile government. Leo's dream paintings became lifelike compared to his earlier works. He painted luminous dragons, beloved characters from forgotten folklore, in a world teeming with potential. The more he painted, the stronger his dreams became, pulling more fiercely at the seams of reality. His creations were no longer confined to canvas; they evolved, manifested, and thrived in the real world. As Leo's prowess grew, so did the influence of the Oraculum. The hostile government, witnessing this rising tide of rebellion, clamped down with a merciless iron grip. The government responded fiercely. Its forces began infiltrating the headquarters, but they were met with living dreams. Leo's luminescent dragons torched their weapons; the folklore defended the Oraculum with a fury borne out of countless generations. Nevertheless, not all battles were waged on fields; some were fought within the psyche. The government's chief captured Leo and subjected him to a device that was to neutralize his dreams. Strapped to the cold steel, Leo felt his dreams being drained and his vivid imagination turning gray. However, dreams, being the intricate labyrinths they are, are not easily conquered. Leo's mind called upon the Oraculum and the world he'd dreamed into life. A surge of shared dreams flooded back into Leo, more vibrant and fearsome than ever. With this, he broke free and shattered the device. It triggered an explosion of dreams so potent that it permanently tore down the boundaries between dreams and reality. The gray meta-reality was replaced with a kaleidoscope of fantastical elements from everybody's dreams. The hostile government was overwhelmed and defeated by their dominant Aeon. The world had changed forever. Dreams, once a forbidden luxury, became the core of existence. The irony sung its terrifyingly beautiful song as the erstwhile oppressive government was swallowed by the dream revolution it aimed to squash. Leo returned to his canvas, once again plunging into another masterpiece. But this time, he was no longer a struggling artist trapped in a dystopian future. He was a dreamer, a revolutionary, and the architect of a world where dreams didn't just come to life; they were life. Reality became the canvas where dreams painted their vivid play, paving the way for an ever-unfolding, thrilling odyssey: Navigating the Mystical Labyrinth of Dreams and Reality. End`,
    playable: true,
  },
  {
    id: "labyrinth-dreams-reality",
    title: "Labyrinth Of Dreams And Reality",
    teaser: "Finding the path through wonder and waking life.",
    body: `Underneath the vast sapphire sky, nestled in the heart of the scenic countryside, sat a humble bakery. It belonged to a rather ordinary-looking man named Thomas. This small establishment was Thomas's fortress, a sanctuary shielding him from the bustling chaos of the cities he once knew, providing an escape into solitude and serenity. As the shroud of darkness started embracing the city, Thomas, a solitary figure leaning against the window of his apartment, began his nightly ritual. Each night, he would surrender himself to a unique realm where dreams intertwined with reality. It was a realm presided over by a mysterious woman, delicate as spun gold yet commanding, with sapphire eyes that rivaled the celestial bodies. But little did he realize that life was about to spring a rather ambiguous yet beautifully entangled adventure on him that would seamlessly blend his dreams and reality. Thomas, a brave-hearted explorer, was entangled in what resembled an enigmatic charade, a labyrinth that hid the secrets everyone had been seeking but none had ever found. This labyrinth was no ordinary structure; it was architectured with shadows and echoes, hidden riddles, and characters that seemed as if they were weaved out of truth and illusion. Compelling as much as it was challenging, his journey was like navigating through a forbidden manuscript deeply hidden in the cosmos. Every night, Thomas would dream of a captivating woman: her sapphire eyes sparkling like the night sky, her hair cascading like a river of liquid gold, and her laughter bearing the delicate charm of the sweetest melody. He named her Seraphina, the angel of his dreams. Her essence was woven into every dream, filling his slumberous mind with love and enchantment. Her cryptic hints morphed into guiding lights, pushing boundaries and enabling Thomas to navigate the maze of the mind. To the first riddle, under the radiant lady's gaze, he teetered on the edges of logic and intuition, finally murmuring the answer into the winds: "An alarm clock." In the realm of sleep, she was draped in celestial sapphire shadows that mirrored her irises. Her golden hair would cascade in waves, sparkling with flecks of stardust and flowing like a river of molten gold. She guided him through the dream labyrinth—an intricate maze of crystal walls and ethereal light—with an uncanny grace that seemed almost supernatural. Every corner of the labyrinth whispered enchanting tales into his ear, each tale carrying a riddle within its echo, veiled in the whispering winds. "What stays where it is when it goes o ff ?" one whisper echoed. Another chimed, "The more you take, the more you leave behind." As Thomas ventured through the winding corridors of puzzles and lore, it was her—the radiant lady with the golden cloak who danced with the shadows between the lone pillars—who often came to his aid. The labyrinth shivered with approval; doors to new perceptions swung open, revealing pathways previously unseen, adorned with the glimmers of the twilight. To the next, as the labyrinth eagerly listened, he answered, "Footsteps." The labyrinth heaved, revealing a staircase spiraling upwards towards the palette of the twilight sky, tinged in hues of mysteries yet to be revealed. But with every riddle solved, he felt the labyrinth growing deeper and grander, enveloping him in its arms, adorned with enigmas and trials. Yet he pressed on, driven by enchantment and challenge. Where the labyrinth ended, or if it ever did truly end, Thomas never knew. But in the process, he learned to harmonize with its rhythm, dance with its riddles, and grace its enigmatic beauty. Each corridor became a picture, each riddle a song, and each encounter with the radiant lady a sonnet that carried him further into the twilight depths. His journey had become an eternal dance between the shadows and the stars. For Thomas, navigating through this compelling labyrinth was no longer about finding the exit; it was about thriving in the existence of the maze itself. The labyrinth was his mystery, his confidant, his life's compelling tale, bathed in the twilight, whispered, and hunting—calling him ever deeper into its grand enigma. Regrettably, with dawn's advent, reality would jarringly pull Thomas away from the labyrinth and the comforting presence of the majestic dream weaver. His mundane life as a book shop owner was in stark contrast to his nightly escapades. The beautifully chaotic world of paper and ink had its charm, but it could never rival the allure of the dream realm. His only solace was the trace of the dream labyrinth he found within the pages of his books. The tales of fantasy, the legends of superheroes and fairies, even the prose of love and loss—all of them held fragments of his dreams. It was in those book-bound mazes that he found a bridge between his dream and reality. One morning, Thomas woke from a dream of Seraphina, his heart throbbing as if he had truly spent the night in her company. Later, as he kneaded his daily bread, he saw a single golden curl resting on his flour-dusted apron, similar to Seraphina's hair. Thomas's dream world and reality began to merge, much like an eclipse, blurring his perception of both worlds again. Each night he dreamt, he found new truths to uncover in his waking hours—hidden books, whispered secrets, people met coincidentally. And each day he navigated reality, he discovered new passages to explore in his dream labyrinth: a forgotten corridor, a hidden room, an untouched labyrinthine garden. World by world, step by step, he was drawing the mysterious lady closer into his existence. The small hint of her presence in his reality shook him to his core once more, blurring the lines between his dreams and his world ever so slightly. As days passed, these signs increased. He saw her eyes in the sky, heard her laughter in the rustle of the wheat fields, and smelled her fragrance in the wildflowers. His peaceful life wasn't so peaceful anymore. It had become an exciting quest, one where he was seeking a dream in his reality, venturing into a thrilling labyrinth of love and enigma. One unforgettable night, under the brilliant moonlight, he dreamed of dancing with Seraphina under a blanket of twinkling stars. The following day at the market, he heard a melody that perfectly matched the one from his dream. It led him through a maze of stalls, pulling him with an irresistible string of enchantment towards a courtyard, where an alluring woman was performing a captivating dance. Her appearance was a striking realization of Seraphina's image. Her dancing reflected the love-infused rhythm of his dreamy night, opening up a realm where dream and reality weren't separate. This was Seraphina, not a figment of his imagination but a living, breathing embodiment of his dreams. He stood at the edge of his dream-made reality, captivated by her presence. Mustering his courage, he pushed open the hitherto closed doors of his solitude and cautiously approached her. To his dazed astonishment, Seraphina recognized him. She had been experiencing the same vivid dreams, in which she called him her "star-kissed baker." Their dreams had painted a portrait of love long before reality could knit them together. The dreamy countryside had suddenly transformed into a canvas, portraying the dreamed love into reality. The humble bakery was no longer just a fortress of solitude but a beacon guiding their entangled fate. And Thomas was no longer a mere character but an adventure-seeking traveler navigating through the thrilling labyrinth of dreams and reality. In the end, their love unfolded like the most beautiful dream—a dream that could not be distinguished from waking life. The journey had ended for Thomas, not in terms of his lifelong passion for dreams but more subtly, a thrill, adventure, and love had now been found in his reality beside the woman with sapphire eyes and golden hair. Love was the bridge that connected his dreams to reality—the route through the thrilling labyrinth. It brought the woman of his dreams into his reality and transformed it into another fantastical journey. Thus, Thomas, the book shop owner, found himself living the dream in reality, his competence in navigating the thrilling labyrinth of dreams and reality taken to an enlightening new level. The line between fantasy and reality had blurred completely, creating a splendid world for both of them. Thus began the life of the 'Star-kissed Baker' and his dreamy angel, narrating an ine ff able tale of a beautiful confluence of dreams and reality. So, dear reader, next time you find yourself lost in a vivid dreamscape, remember Thomas and Seraphina's tale. Who knows which turn of the labyrinth might guide your dreams into a thrilling reality, spilling magic in the monotony, and taking you on an unforgettable adventure? End`,
    playable: true,
  },
  {
    id: "dreamcatcher-necklace",
    title: "Tales Of Ivy's Dreamcatcher Necklace",
    teaser: "Ivy's dreamcatcher necklace and the stories it holds.",
    body: `In the small sleepy town of Dreams Veil, England, lived a young girl named Ivy. The city was known for its mystery, a legend where dreams would come to life. Ivy was a special kind of dreamer, her dreams were always vivid and vibrant.She was an artist and her large canvas was her dream. Night after night she slept to see great ideas and woke to cover her canvas with exotic colours, fantastic figures, and enchanting landscapes drawn from her dreams. What made Ivy unique, however, was a touch of faith, a belief that her dreams were more than just illusions. Every night, Ivy snuggled under her duvet, clutching her dream catcher necklace. She fervently wished her dreams would come true. The necklace, a delicate cobweb of silver and crystal beads, was a beloved gift from her grandmother, an eccentric and enchanting woman known as the village dream weaver. Once the time shifted from night to sunset, Ivy's routine began. A small prayer, a light kiss on the central gemstone of the pendant, a whisper of her dreams in the cool night air, and finally she closed her eyes and let her thoughts drift away. Being a wildly imaginative young girl, Ivy's dreams were vivid and packed with mystery. She dreamed of adventures and quests, mythical creatures, all waiting to be discovered in the depths of her mind. From befriending mermaids in the turquoise sea to being serenaded by a group of goblins to drinking tea with a Jabberwocky, her dreams formed the tapestry to her fortress of solitude. Nothing happened for years. Ivy's dreams were confined to her sleep and never entered the realm of reality. They were fanciful figments of her sleep-induced creativity, an everyday feature of the human mind. But even as time passed, Ivy did not waver, as she clung to her dream catcher every night and whispered every desire into the inky darkness, hoping that one night the loops and spirals of her chain would envelop her wish and bring it to life. But then one evening, bathed in silver moonlight, Ivy's routine paid o ff . As her words disappeared into the air, a mu ffl ed rustle replaced the silence. The dreamcatcher began to emit a soft glow as the spider webs weaved new patterns. Startled yet hopeful, Ivy watched, her heart beating with anticipation. When she woke up, she found herself in a green meadow with daisies and lilies in full bloom and the sun like a painting in the sky. When she looked around, she recognized it. It was her dream, woven into reality. A vibrant butterfly, just like one from one of her dreams, fluttered around. Her heart pounded in joyful disbelief, and she reached out to let the butterfly land on her palm. From that day on, Ivy's wishes began to come true. Every night the dream catcher wove a dream—a reality that Ivy woke up to. Her dreams were no longer locked up for the nights; they escaped the confines of her mind to exist in the light of day. Ivy's nights under her duvet turned into the stepping stones of her magical life. Ivy lived a life painted by the hues of her dreams, with each routine leading her to charming adventures. So she was no longer just Ivy; she had become Ivy, the dream weaver, living in the reality of the dreams she once desired. So every night, Ivy snuggled under her duvet, clutching her dream catcher necklace, fervently wishing for her dreams to come to life, knowing that they absolutely would. One day, Ivy had an unusually vivid dream about a magical realm. There was a towering canvas that reached up to the clouds, where starry-eyed elves fluttered about, dipping their tiny brushes into pots of multicolored rainbows and painting spots of life. She was enchanted by this colossal cloth, every inch of which radiated an infectious warmth of magic. The next morning, Ivy felt an unknown urge. That dream wasn't just a dream; it was a call. She longed to make sense of it and decided it was time to paint it—the great canvas of her dreams—a masterpiece of pure faith. Ivy was an artist, a dreamer, and an extraordinary believer in the uniqueness of every moment. There was an old saying among the townspeople: "If Ivy can't paint it, it's not worth seeing!" And true to her reputation, she captured life in both its grossest and most sublime forms, interweaving reality with the vast world of her imagination. For years, Ivy had imagined a painting—a great masterpiece—that represented her deepest dreams and her wildest beliefs. This was not a whimsical wish; she waited for the right moment and the right inspiration. She wanted this work of art to be the culmination of her creative journey. Finally, after walking the path of life, love, and endless dreams, Ivy finally decided the moment had come. She invested in a giant canvas—an empty space that echoed her dreams. As she placed it on the easel, she felt a thrill of anticipation gleam in her eyes. It was a pure white landscape, untouched, like freshly fallen snow, waiting for the footsteps of a traveler. It was a symbol of pure faith, of expected miracles, and of dreams not yet dreamed. Ivy's usual routine took a back seat. Every day, she lost herself in the whirlwind of colors and the dance of her brush. Hours became days, days became weeks, and gradually the bond between Ivy and her canvas became an ethereal a ff air. Every stroke of her brush seemed to create a new universe, with every speck of color filling the canvas with its own story. The grandeur of her imagination slowly but surely took shape. Time was irrelevant in her art studio. It transformed into a timeless world, vibrant with Ivy's artistic spirit. The masterpiece she was working on wasn't just any painting; it was the essence of Ivy herself. She painted her dreams, her hopes, her beliefs, her fears, and her love. Every emotion she had ever experienced found a place in it, like the pieces of an intricate puzzle forming a reathtakingly beautiful picture. The once blank canvas was now a glorious montage of Ivy's life. Her dreams soared over the canvas like a majestic eagle; the delicate blossoms of hope bloomed in the corners, while the mighty mountains of resilience stood in their matchless glory. Her fears were like shadows, casting an underlying darkness that emphasized the vibrancy of her life. Love flowed like a serene river, meandering through her experiences and beliefs. After weeks of dedicated work, Ivy beheld her masterpiece. The large canvas burst with life, and the brushstrokes spoke volumes about her spirit. It wasn't just a work of art; it was her message to the world, a testament to her belief in the immense power of dreams and the power of the human spirit to make those dreams come true. When Ivy decided to exhibit her canvas to the public, the townspeople witnessed a miracle unfold. The great masterpiece was not just a painting; it was a reflection of Ivy's heart. Each observer saw a piece of themselves in Ivy's art because wasn't each of their lives a grand canvas full of dreams, hopes, fears, and love? Ivy's art was well celebrated around Dreams Veil, but news of her canvas of dreams was met with skepticism. The towering canvas was too ambitious, they said. Ivy faced countless challenges, but her quiet belief kept her going. She worked day and night, passionately pouring her soul into the canvas and feeding it with her dreams. However, the physical world presented limitations, and Ivy was unable to recreate the ethereal beauty she had imagined in her mind. Ivy was dejected; her dream seemed impossible to achieve. But her dreamcatcher necklace was a memory—a call to believe. She decided to do the unthinkable. She would sleep under her unfinished canvas, live in her dreams, and imbue life there. Her world consisted of color, texture, emotion, and imagination, translated onto canvas. Ivy's painting was not just a hobby; it was her life force, her language, and her existential anchor. Latent in her color world was a strange artifact: a dreamcatcher necklace that Ivy always wore. The dream catcher, an intricate weft of vibrant threads decorated with delicate feathers, was not just an ornament for Ivy. The interconnected fabric was symbolic of her life; the dreams intertwined with periods of reality. She said to her friends: "Wearing my dream catcher, I embrace my dreams." The necklace was an enchanting blend of her heritage and her creative inclination—an echo of an origin that drew strength from dreams and faith. It was given to her by her grandmother and had a remarkable charm and a formidable history. Native to American Native culture, dream catchers were seen as protective charms, trapping nightmares in their fabric and allowing only pleasant dreams to filter in. To Ivy, her necklace was much more than a simple trinket; it was a memory, a promise, and a beacon that illuminated her creative pursuits. Every night, she retreated beneath an unfinished canvas. She wanted to live with her thoughts and her dreams and let her intuition succeed. She painted not only with shades but also with her faith and ambition, which she carefully preserved in the woven symbol around her neck. There were days when inspiration would elude Ivy, and factions of reality would poke giant holes in the fabric of the dreamcatcher. Doubt, fear, and disappointment would slip through the protected network while the real essence, the dreams themselves, languished, trapped in the labyrinth of everyday struggles. But even through the disappointment, the dream catcher around Ivy's neck was a constant reminder of her indomitable spirit. Her heart beat with the rhythm of a dreamer. Ivy gripped her brush tighter, her eyes reflecting the fire within. She presented herself at the altar of her cloth and let the pain seep into color; her blows came alive, frenzied but purposeful. It was these moments of internal chaos that led to the birth of a masterpiece that Ivy cherished most—a disaster turned into liberation. On such days, the dream catcher necklace was a reassurance, whispering quietly in her ears, "Faith. 'Create.' And she did. She created not just art, but an entire universe within her canvas. Each painting became a testament to her resilience and a monument to her dreams, and each stroke was a story waiting to be embraced. Ivy's dreamcatcher necklace was a symbol of her interpreter and defender of the unspoken whims of her mind. It was more than a memory; it was calling. A call to create, to dream, and to believe in the incredible power of imagination She slept under her unfinished canvas, lived in her dreams, and immediately gave them life there. For every captured dream, a masterpiece was born, a sign of a woman and her ethereal belief in the magic of dreams. As she fell asleep beneath her creation that night, the tangible world and the dream realm began to mingle. The starry-eyed elves from her dreams fluttered around her, their magical brushes infused with ethereal colors, as she painted vibrant bolts on the towering canvas. There was enchantment in the air, and the canvas began to glow, each stroke pulsating with energy. When Ivy woke up the next morning, she was shocked. Her canvas was a mirror image of the magical realm she dreamed of, but it was more than just a painting. The starry-eyed elves fluttered around, dropping colors onto the canvas. Her dream had come to life. Ivy found herself surprised when she woke up to a soft beam of sunlight streaming in through the small crack of her old, modest window. Her heart pounded with excitement and disbelief as she stared at the easel across the room. Her small, modest studio, which usually exuded the scent of watercolors and oil paints, now exuded an aura of enchantment. She shook her head, rubbed her eyes, and peered at the canvas again. Her eyes widened, and a smile appeared at the corners of her mouth. What she saw was unlikely, but it was real. On the canvas was a beautiful painting—a mirror image of her dream from the previous night. The lush, cascading fields of emerald green grass glow in the shimmering reflection of the winding river. The beautiful trees with radiant leaves patterned like the finest lace, the flowers glittering with the stardust of countless otherworldly colors It was the ethereal realm that her subconscious had concocted and, to her surprise, her hands had flawlessly mimicked. Yet that wasn't the magical part, because Ivy had painted her dreams many times. The magic was that this realm was literally alive. The painted grass rustled as if a gentle breeze had passed by. The radiant leaves trembled gently, and every now and then a leaf fluttered across the painted canvas. The flowers would experience a soft pixi glow, and the river would ripple and turn. It was as if Ivy's vivid dreams were not limited to her sleep but had found a portal to reality through her uninhibited creativity. Excitement and fear began to wrestle in Ivy's mind. Yet she found herself enchanted and drawn to the live painting. She soon found her trembling fingers reaching for the canvas and hovering just inches from the painted river. Gathering her courage, she touched the blue swirls and gasped as a cool feeling passed through her fingers. It was as if she could actually feel the flowing river! Still in disbelief, Ivy spent the rest of her day looking at her magical cloth, living proof of her dream and creativity. Some call it a mystery; others call it magic. But for Ivy, it was the bridge between her dreams and reality. And as night began to fall, Ivy wondered: If she dreams again tonight, would those dreams also come to life on this wondrous canvas? The quiet mystery of the unknown made Ivy's heart race as she clung to the idea of endless possibilities. That night, Ivy slept in anticipation, her dreams filled with magical realms waiting to be painted. As for the mysterious cloth, it continued to fascinate, creating an enchanting realm that echoed the whispers of Ivy's dreams and became reality. And so, Ivy's studio was no longer just a painting room but a gateway to her magical dreamscape. The news spread like wildfire. People from nearby towns gathered to witness the spectacle. The cynics were speechless, amazed by the enchanting reality. Children squealed with delight as they chased the starry-eyed elves as the colors flowed from the canvas, causing flowers to bloom and animals to dance, infusing the city with life and magic. Ivy watched the scene with misty eyes. Her faith had paid o ff . Dreams Veil was never the same again. It was what the name suggested: a veil of realized dreams. Ivy's faith changed not only her life but also the city forever. She proved that sweet dreams come true, and the only requirement is that you believe in them fervently. "Her brush was her wand, the colors of her spell, and Ivy, the sorceress who wove dreams into reality. "The canvas of dreams stood like a beacon, reminding all that dreams were not merely figments of the sleeping mind but cradles of magic waiting to be brought to life." "All it took was a little faith." End`,
    playable: true,
  },
  {
    id: "spiritual-odyssey",
    title: "Transformation Of A Spiritual Odyssey",
    teaser: "A touch of transformation on an inner journey.",
    body: `Once, in the tranquil confines of a small mid-western town named Harmonyville, there lived a boy named Elliot. Elliot was a child of prodigious talent, blessed with the Midas touch. From his earliest days, whatever he laid his hands on seemed to transform miraculously. A sapling that refused to bloom would burst with plump fruit. A fiddler who couldn't carry a tune would find perfect pitch with his counsel. A rusted tool, reshaped by his hands, would regain its lost sheen and purpose. Despite the extraordinary flair, a void lingered within Elliot, beckoning him to explore far beyond his material prowess. One day, his grandmother told him stories of guardian angels—invisible, ethereal celestial beings that watched over and guided the children of the earth. This sparked something within Elliot. He longed to communicate with his personal guardian angels, hoping to discover what was missing from within his heart. Elliot began his journey with intense study, poring over sacred texts and tomes, searching for means to converse with his unseen sentinels. In ancient scriptures, he discovered Angelarium, the language of the angels. So he undertook the considerable task of learning this divine language. For moons and seasons, Elliot dedicated his days and nights to the Angelarium. Though his learning turned his Midas touch dormant, the hope of filling the void within fueled his commitment. Then a day dawned, and with the first sunbeam of the morning, he found himself fluently speaking the ethereal language, whispering praises and prayers into the morning sky. Now, equipped with the medium of divine communication, Elliot sought solitude in the heart of Harmonyville's forgotten forest. Underneath an ancient oak, he began a steady colloquy, weaving prayers like beautiful tapestries of faith and hope into the air. Days turned into nights, nights into days, but his guardian angels remained in a searing silence. Just when Elliot was about to crumble under disappointment, he felt a gust of wind caressing the forest leaves—sunlight streaked through branches, casting a dance of shadows and light. An ethereal resonance echoed in the wind, and like a river stream, words of Angelarium flowed back to him. His guardian angels spoke, voices soft as a summer breeze yet as profound as the deepest ocean. They revealed to him that his unique gifts were a form of divine love, with each transformation an echo of celestial harmony on Earth. Yet they sensed his deep longing for something beyond his physical abilities. They told him, "Elliot, the void you feel is not a lack, but a space for you to sculpt your own identity beyond the boundaries of your talents." Emotions welled up within Elliot. The angels' words kindled an ember within him, a spark of self-realization. overwhelmed yet enlightened, he asked, "How?" The angels replied, "Be kind, be compassionate, and be humble." Let these virtues define you more than your ability to transform. Engage with the world, not just to improve it materially but also spiritually. Guide those who stray, help those who fall, and love those who are unloved. This will guide you to seek your true self." As the divine voices faded, Elliot felt a surge of warmth flooding his soul. He realized that his search had finally led him to his destiny. His amazing talents were not a definition of his worth but tools to give to the world. From then on, Elliot lived his life as the angels had suggested. His transformational touch returned, now imbued with a deeper purpose. More than anything, he valued simplicity, kindness, and humility—virtues that eventually filled the void within him. Throughout his life, he sought to touch not just objects but hearts, to transform not just the physical but the spiritual, and to uplift the world not just with his gifts but with his virtues. His guardian angels had indeed guided him well. Thus, the boy with the Midas touch transformed himself and found his true identity, leaving a legacy not of golden objects but of golden deeds in the heart of Harmonyville. He was not just Elliot—the boy who could transform anything he touched—but Elliot, the boy who transformed for the better, anyone he met. End`,
    playable: true,
  },
  {
    id: "longest-journey",
    title: "The Longest Journey Within Oneself",
    teaser: "The longest road is the one that leads within.",
    body: `In the sleepy, tranquil town of El Derville, there lived a curious and adventurous boy named Ethan. El Derville was surrounded by a thick, mystical forest named the Eldwoods. These mystical woods were believed to house ethereal beings, whom the townsfolk considered their guardian angels. The elderly often told tales of these guardian angels, whispering into the ears of fascinated children as the fire crackled in the hearth. Ethan, though young, had the heart of an explorer, always absorbed in the whirlpool of his own thoughts and questions. Though he loved listening to the stories of the guardian angels, he developed a peculiar obsession. Ethan didn't just want to listen to stories about them; he yearned to meet these heavenly beings himself and embarked on a journey deep into the heart of the Eldwoods, praying and hoping that these beings would be his guide. As the new moon rose high in the black velvet sky, loud whispers echoed through Elderville. Ethan was missing, and his journey into the Eldwoods had begun. However, it was no regular search for an elusive mythical creature or a secret treasure; this journey was about seeking, understanding, and finding oneself. Ethan's journey through the mystical woods was a daunting one. Formerly harmless creatures seemed more threatening; every rustling leaf made his heart leap, and shadows appeared as menacing figures looming in the darkness. But it wasn't the dangers lurking in the forest that scared Ethan; he constantly found himself battling his own insecurities and fear of failure. Was he foolish for believing in guardian angels? Would he ever discover the deeper meaning of life he sought? Yet he forged ahead, driven by an indomitable spirit. He was guided by a shimmering, ethereal glow that led him through the perils of the forest. It was his faith in the guardian angels that lent him strength and courage. He was determined and continued his journey, steeped in silent prayers that his guardian angels were listening to. Days turned into weeks, and weeks into months. The forest drew him deeper into its heart, and he, too, found himself delving deeper into his own thoughts, his subconscious. He wrestled with his fears and insecurities, arguing with himself and the shadows. His thoughts became more profound; the lad realized he was not just grappling with tangible trees and creatures but with intangible feelings, thoughts, and complexes, struggling to understand the essence of who he truly was. He was guided by a shimmering, ethereal glow that led him through the perils of the forest. It was his faith in the guardian angels that lent him strength and courage. He was determined and continued his journey, steeped in silent prayers that his guardian angels were listening to. Days turned into weeks, and weeks into months. The forest drew him deeper into its heart, and he, too, found himself delving deeper into his own thoughts, his subconscious. He wrestled with his fears and insecurities, arguing with himself and the shadows. His thoughts became more profound; the lad realized he was not just grappling with tangible trees and creatures but with intangible feelings, thoughts, and complexes, struggling to understand the essence of who he truly was. And then, one day, something remarkable happened. As he sat up on a hill overlooking Elderville, he found his mind detached from his body, looking down upon it. It was an out-of-body experience where his thoughts held a silent conversation with his heart and soul. Ethan felt a wave of tranquility wash over him. He saw before him not a simple boy from Elderville but a youthful spirit embodied in a strong and brave heart, a heart forever yearning for knowledge, a soul whose questions echoed louder than its answers. He felt a profound connection with his inner self and finally grasped his purpose. He was not just a boy seeking guardian angels; he was a young spirit on a journey to find himself. Returning to Elderville, Ethan carried with him the wisdom of the guardian angels, not as ethereal beings but as a guiding light within his own heart. That newfound realization reshaped him from an inquisitive boy into a wise young man. And then, one day, something remarkable happened. As he sat up on a hill overlooking Elderville, he found his mind detached from his body, looking down upon it. It was an out-of-body experience where his thoughts held a silent conversation with his heart and soul. Ethan felt a wave of tranquility wash over him. He saw before him not a simple boy from Elderville but a youthful spirit embodied in a strong and brave heart, a heart forever yearning for knowledge, a soul whose questions echoed louder than its answers. He felt a profound connection with his inner self and finally grasped his purpose. He was not just a boy seeking guardian angels; he was a young spirit on a journey to find himself. Returning to Elderville, Ethan carried with him the wisdom of the guardian angels, not as ethereal beings but as a guiding light within his own heart. That newfound realization reshaped him from an inquisitive boy into a wise young man. His journey became an age-old tale of self-discovery, etched in Elderville's folklore. The tale of the brave Ethan who ventured into the Redwoods to speak to the guardian angels, only to realize that the journey was a path leading him inward—towards understanding his true essence. His story transformed him into an embodiment of courage, wisdom, and self-discovery, inspiring many to embark on their own journey towards self-realization. Thus, Ethan learned that our guardian angels are not always beings dwelling in concealed corners of mysterious forests but are often the hidden strengths and guiding forces residing within ourselves. It taught him the most significant lesson: the longest journey one takes is not around the world but within oneself. End`,
    playable: true,
  },
  {
    id: "self-discovery",
    title: "A Journey Towards Self-Discovery",
    teaser: "A boy named Sky — growing into who he truly is.",
    body: `Once upon a time, in a quaint little town named Eldenwood, a young boy named Sky. He was known for his piercing blue eyes, as azure as the sky itself, and a head full of blond hair that danced in the wind like an ethereal flame. An inception of curiosity enveloped his spirit, instigating an insatiable thirst for the unknown and a desire to venture into the profound depths of self—past, present, and future. One moonlit night, Sky found himself gazing at the multitude of stars webbing the vast canvas of the cosmos. He longed to explore the galaxies, meet new creatures, and learn from them. But more importantly, he craved to unlock the mysteries of his existence and to converse with the guardian angels who could guide him on a journey to self-discovery. "Dear guardian angels," he murmured under his breath. "Guide me to my true self." The next morning—an ordinary day at first glance—heeding his grandmother's call, Sky went into the dense forest behind their home to collect mushrooms. Amidst the labyrinth of towering trees and vibrant foliage, he suddenly spotted unusual footprints. A strange anticipation welled up in him. Could this be a sign from the angels he had implored at midnight? Driven by his curiosity, Sky decided to follow the footprints. They led him deeper into the woods until he stumbled upon a fair-haired stranger dressed in radiant white robes. "You asked for guidance, Sky." The stranger spoke, his voice a melodious echo that resonated in pristine silence. Gasping in astonishment, Sky nodded, fervently inquiring, "Are you my guardian angel?" The stranger merely smiled, casting a look as ancient and profound as the cosmos itself. a beckoning gesture from the enigmatic figure, and they embarked on an incredible journey. They traversed the singing streams, crossed the glistening desert, climbed the mighty mountains, and navigated the wild, blue yonder. Everywhere they went, they met people of di ff erent cultures and creatures of various kinds. Sky saw kindness and brutality, courage and cowardice, love and hate, joy and sorrow. The journey was beautiful, terrifying, and enlightening—a roller coaster ride of emotions and experiences. Every night, they sat by the campfire as the stranger—whom Sky had begun addressing as Alaric—shared stories about bravery, integrity, wisdom, and the divine balance between good and evil. He listened, rapt with attention, absorbing each tale, learning from them, reflecting on his actions, and questioning his core beliefs. Along the way, he had experienced terrifying storms, loneliness, exhaustion, and hunger. But it had also been enriching, giving him wisdom and the abilities to understand, empathize, love, and discern. He had learned to appreciate solitude, the elegance of silence, the beauty of nature, and the value of companionship, courage, and persistence. On the final leg of their wanderings, Alaric brought him back to Eldenwood. As they stood near the town's boundary, Alaric turned to Sky with a benevolent smile. "Your journey towards self-discovery isn't over yet, Sky." Sky looked at Alaric, his eyes reflecting gratitude for his guidance, comprehension, and experiences. He knew his journey had just begun—the quest for knowledge was endless, and all the answers lay within him. He had discovered a part of himself but vowed to keep seeking, understanding, and growing. With a happy heart, Sky watched as Alaric disappeared into the woods, leaving him with incredible memories of their journey and invaluable life lessons. In the end, it was not about becoming someone new or di ff erent. It was about finding, understanding, and accepting oneself, embracing life with its ups and downs. Life was a journey laced with lessons, and Sky was eager to etch his story in the sands of time. He walked back into Eldenwood, not merely as a boy with a name but as Sky—a young man on a perpetual journey towards self-discovery and enlightenment. End`,
    playable: true,
  },
  {
    id: "tangled-threads",
    title: "Tangled In The Threads Of Life",
    teaser: "Life's tangled threads, gently sorted by courage.",
    body: `As the golden sunset reached beyond the edge of the horizon, bright strokes of fiery red and blissful purple painted the canvas of the sky. Nathan sat under the tranquility of the weeping willow in his backyard, staring vacantly at the endless expanse above. He could only utter a single question, murmuring into the ambiance: "Why have I been tangled in this puzzle of life?" Nathan's melancholy found solace in the quiet rustlings of nighttime. He had lived what seemed like countless others' lifetimes; born amidst the dirty, grimy backdrop of the city, he strived to secure a single job, to earn a slice of bread or find a spot under a sturdy roof. He had known poverty and hardship closely. Then he climbed the trusty ladder of the corporate hierarchy, achieved success, and tasted bountiful fortunes only a few could dream of. But the expensive Scotch couldn't take the edge o ff his rumbling mind, nor could the gleaming trophies fill the void in his heart. On one cold winter night, when the mundane realities of life were hitting a bit too hard, he felt a sudden rush of warm energy surrounding him. From the frost-tinted window, a dazzling celestial being descended, stringing along a melody that made the blinds of frozen snow melt away. An angel, tall and ethereal, her wings shaded with hues of compassion, reached out to him. "Nathan, we invite you to a paradise within you, where sweet dreams borne from pure hearts and hopeful minds reside." Intrigued and desperate, Nathan agreed to the angel's offer, stepping into an unusually welcoming whirlwind. He was carried away, far from the snowy city landscape. When he opened his eyes, he found himself on a deserted island. A paradise it was, with lush, eternal green and sparkling sands that danced along the tune of the turquoise waves. But the catch? The paradise was empty, an echo of beautiful emptiness. The angel's soft voice resonated, "Here in this paradise, Nathan, every grain of sand represents the choices you've made, the people you've met, and the dreams you've pursued." Overwhelmed by his tangible past and vivid memories, Nathan was lost in the labyrinth of his decisions. He realized the true meaning of paradise—it was a reflection of his life, his choices, and his dreams. Not all were radiant; some were murky, weighed by regret and lost chances. But as the days rolled into nights and the shadows melted into dawn, Nathan began to comprehend the crux of it all. He was tangled because he had entwined his worth with his failures, forgetting that they were just life lessons. The paradise was not a prison but a mirror, a mirror right into his heart and soul. It was a realm where every dream and desire reflected their true colors. Regret was merely a sand grain, swiftly carried away by the tide, leaving fresh imprints behind. Nathan understood that life comes with its tangles, but once the knots were untied patiently, they became straight threads, weaving the fabric of lessons and lending strength. Embracing his past and seeping wisdom from his failures, he realized that he was not a tragedy of errors but a symphony of learning arcs. Life offered more than just trivial pursuits. It was stories and sonnets, a splash of colors on the canvas, a musical note in the symphony, a grain of sand on the infinite beach. Each block, each hurdle, and each knot was a twist in the tale, a unique pattern in the wefty tapestry, a twining, looping spin in life's grand ballet. With the angel's blessings, Nathan returned to his world—the chaos, the stark realities—no longer a puzzle but a stage, a stage where he would dance amidst the tangles, embracing the turns, the leaps, and the falls with open arms, a hopeful heart, and an enlightened mind. Life was no longer just a rat race for Nathan; it transformed into a mesmerizing dance ceremony, for he was no longer tangled; tangled was the thread of life. End`,
    playable: true,
  },
  {
    id: "finding-love",
    title: "Finding Love In Unexpected Places",
    teaser: "Love found where no one thought to look.",
    body: `Once upon a time, in a small, picturesque town called Willowbrook, there lived a vibrant and imaginative young girl named Emily. Emily had a heart full of kindness, and she believed in the magic of love. She often found herself daydreaming about sweet fairy tales and happily-ever-afters. One sunny afternoon, as Emily ventured into the enchanting Willowbrook Woods, she stumbled upon an abandoned treehouse hidden among the tall trees. Curiosity sparked in her eyes, and she decided to investigate further. Climbing up the creaky ladder, Emily found herself transported to a world of wonder, where she discovered the books left behind by the once imaginative treehouse owners. Excitement twinkled in Emily's eyes as she picked up an old, tattered book called "Sweet Dreams Do Come True." The book was filled with delightful stories of love found in the most unexpected places. It spoke of love that bloomed between the unlikeliest of individuals, and Emily's young heart skipped a beat as she absorbed the magical tales. She imagined what it would be like to experience such a heartwarming story herself. Little did she know, destiny was about to grant her wish. Days turned into weeks, and Willowbrook was preparing for its annual summer fair. Emily's heart fluttered with anticipation, as she believed this could be the time for her own unexpected encounter. Her hopes were pinned on the magic she read in the book. One summer evening, as the fair lights twinkled in the distance, Emily heard a soft melody emanating from a hidden corner of the park. She followed the enchanting music until she stumbled upon a young boy named Ethan, playing his guitar with passion and talent. The two struck up a conversation, and it was as if they had known each other for years. Emily discovered that Ethan, too, had an imagination that surpassed reality. They shared stories, dreams, and even the tales from "Sweet Dreams Do Come True." Their connection was undeniable. As the weeks turned into months, Emily and Ethan spent every opportunity together, exploring the treehouse, creating new adventures, and making memories that would last a lifetime. They laughed, they shared secrets, and they whispered about their dreams. One splendid day, against the backdrop of the setting sun, Ethan took Emily by the hand and led her to a hidden meadow. Surrounded by blooming flowers and the sweet scent of summer, he declared his love for her in a way that mirrored the most heartfelt tales they had read overwhelmed with joy. Emily realized that love had indeed found her in the most unexpected of places—in her newfound friendship with Ethan. And so, their love story flourished, just like the pages of the book they had cherished. As they grew older, Emily and Ethan found solace in knowing that sometimes love reveals itself when least expected—in the form of a warm smile, a shared interest, or even through the pages of an old yet magical book. In Willowbrook, their love became not just a tale but a symbol of the infinite possibilities of finding love in unexpected places. From that day onward, the abandoned treehouse was no longer abandoned at all. It became a beacon of hope, reminding everyone who visited that the sweetest dreams do come true. As time passed, the whispers of Emily and Ethan's love story filled the hearts of the townsfolk, inspiring them to keep their eyes and hearts open, for love truly has a way of finding its way into the most unassuming places. End`,
    playable: true,
  },
  {
    id: "manifesting-dream",
    title: "Manifesting Your Dream Into Reality",
    teaser: "Turning a quiet wish into something you can hold.",
    body: `In a tiny hamlet named Harmonyville lived a humble boy named Eamon. His life was as ordinary as that of the fellow villagers, with one exception: his dreams. Eamon dreamt big and harbored an unfeigned passion for music. From a pervasive farmer's life to a prestigious concert pianist, Eamon's aspirations were as vast as the ocean and as high as the mountain peaks. Every day, nestled on the rocky edges of Harmonyville, he played an ancient, weather-beaten piano. Eamon's fingers danced over the keys, weaving beautiful symphonies that echoed throughout the vast terrain. His dreams were alive in his melodies but seemed unreachable, trapped within the confines of his pastoral existence. One day, an itinerant sage named Elder Aloysius visited Harmonyville. Gilded with a cloak of wisdom and a warm smile, his reputation preceded him. Aloysius was known as the Dream Whisperer, known for his incredible ability to turn dreams into reality. Seeking guidance, Eamon approached the sage, sharing his aspiration to be a concert pianist. Aloysius looked closely at Eamon and then broke into a knowing smile. Sage Aloysius decided to unravel the cryptic process of manifesting a dream into reality. On a star-kissed night, the first step took place. Eamon was instructed to happily embrace his love for music and concert pianists fully. He would have to believe in his dream and then commit to it. It was not about repressing doubts but facing them squarely and then choosing to persist with a smile. Eamon indulged himself with piano sonatas, concertos, and compositions by the greats, fueling his dream till it blazed like a relentless wildfire within him. Next was the Inscription Series, where Eamon was asked to inscribe his dream on parchments and place them around the village. At first, people took no notice of these papers fluttering in the breeze, but soon their eyes were drawn towards the parchments, as though a magnet pulled them closer. His dream was no longer exclusively his; it was a part of the collective consciousness. Aloysius encouraged him to live out his dream before it manifested. Eamon started performing nightly piano concerts for Harmonyville's inhabitants. Beneath the lantern lights, Eamon played his heart out. His fingers weaved waves of mellifluous notes that resonated in the hearts of his fellow villagers. The following step was steering his moral compass right and mastering the art of patience. Elder Aloysius believed that manifesting a dream is not just about asking but also about being morally suitable for the dream's fruition. The process could either be instantaneous or long-drawn. And so, Eamon became the epitome of patience, perseverance, and morality. Months danced by. No magical event had occurred, as Eamon had hoped. His dream resembled a distant mirage, shimmering yet unreachable. But the determined farmer's boy held on, imprinting the essence of his dream in every note he played. Then the day arrived. An infamous maestro, on his journey across lands, heard the meticulous maestro hidden within Eamon's melodies. Intrigued and impressed, he invited Eamon to join his world-renowned orchestra. His dream, which was a fragile sapling, had now metamorphosed into a formidable tree. Through courage, frequent unwavering visualization, enduring patience, and steadfast morality, Eamon had manifested his dream into reality. The secrets of dream manifestation that Elder Aloysius had decoded had changed Eamon's world. Miracles happen when dreams are pursued with ardent belief and persistent action. In the beautiful realm where dreams and reality dance, the Secrets of "Manifesting Your Creativity" breathe life into aspirations, making them tangible realities. Eamon's tale became folklore. An indelible story of success and wisdom illuminates the mysterious journey of manifesting dreams into life. His music echoed through Harmonyville, reminding everyone that the power of dream realization lies within and that any dream can manifest if nurtured with love, persistence, and unwavering faith. End`,
    playable: true,
  },
  {
    id: "gentle-little-girl-esme",
    title: "The Gentle Little Girl, Whose Name Is Esme",
    teaser: "Little Esme of Loughmore — a gentle heart full of wonder.",
    body: `! Once upon a time, in the small yet bustling town of Loughmore, lived a peculiar little girl, Esme. Esme was unlike any other child; she didn't engage in playful antics or frivolous laughter. Instead, she had an ethereal appeal, a silent aura that whispered secrets only the observant could hear. Esme was perceived as strange, but the tenderness that illuminated her face when she smiled portrayed an otherwise gentle and beautiful soul. Esme had a special gift; she could heal hearts, not with any supernatural powers but purely with the warmth of her presence. It had grown to become an unstated consensus among the townsfolk that a bad day could only be salvaged if one encountered Esme's warming smile. Regardless of the storm brewing in their lives, the sight of her gentle face calmed their turbulent souls, injecting joy and hope. Loughmore had seen better days before an economic meltdown swathed the town in a shadow of despair. The townsfolk, once the most cheerful lot, had withered under the impact, their smiles replaced by lines of worry and fear. Despite the melancholy ambiance, Esme defied the cheerless trend with her heart-winning smile. She would take up her usual spot by the town's central fountain. Gentle as a dove, Esme would trace patterns on the water's surface or make origami birds from the scrap paper she found. Her joy in these simple pleasures was infectious; her soothing presence was an elixir for the town's collective gloom. One day, a traveling bard named Roderick came to Loughmora. He noticed the unusual quiet of the town but was intrigued by the sight of the little girl humming softly by the fountain, her joy undiminished by the sorrow around her. Roderick, curious and inspired, decided to compose a ballad about Esme. He spent days observing her. He watched the peace she brought to scowling faces and battered hearts. He noticed how her eyes held a sea of kindness and generosity. Finally, after several days, he weaved together a beautiful ballad, "The Gentle Enigma." With a lute in hand, he began to sing in the town square, his voice carrying the magical tale of Esme. As the poignant melody wafted through the air, the townsfolk began to gather around. The lyrics painted a tale of a fragile girl whose strength lay in her gentle heart; her smile was a beacon of hope amidst a sea of despair. They realized that even in the face of adversity, they had a reason to smile and to be hopeful. Their hearts swelled with gratitude for Esme. They finally understood the silent, strange girl who, with her gentle spirit, contributed to their lives in the most profound way. Soon, the ballad spread beyond Loughmore. The tale of the gentle enigma who could make people smile with her radiance traveled far and wide, touching countless hearts. Esme unknowingly sparked a revolution of joy. It showed that no matter how grim life appears to be, finding joy in little things and sharing smiles can create significant ripples of happiness. With her large, curious eyes and a head full of untamed chestnut curls, Esme stood out amidst the sea of ordinary faces. She possessed a unique aura that radiated from within, capturing hearts e ff ortlessly. In her mere presence, the atmosphere seemed to lighten, and the once dulled colors of Loughmore suddenly brimmed with vibrant beauty. Esme possessed an enduring spirit, one that could not be diminished by life's challenges. She faced each day with unwavering determination, and her boundless energy was contagious. The townsfolk soon found themselves inspired by her optimism, gradually adopting it as their own. It was her warming smile, however, that held the true power of enchantment. It had the ability to melt even the iciest of hearts. As the townspeople went about their daily tasks, Esme would joyfully greet them, her infectious grin promising a momentary escape from the worries that plagued their lives. News of the girl spread like wildfire, and soon visitors flocked to witness her magic firsthand. The town became known as the home of the girl with the warm smile, and people journeyed from far and wide to bask in her presence. Loughmore blossomed into a bustling community, with merchants setting up stalls and entertainers captivating crowds. Esme had sparked an e ff ervescent joy, breathing new life into the once-sleepy town.The townsfolk treasured their gentle enigma, knowing that as long as Esme was there, they could conquer any hardship that came their way. She became their pillar of hope, her mere existence serving as a reminder that they were capable of resilience and strength. Despite her age, Esme possessed an innate wisdom beyond her years. She seemed to understand the complexities of human emotions and was always ready with a comforting word or a tender touch. The people of Loughmore poured their troubles into her ears, and in return, she would offer sage advice that left them feeling lighter. Year after year, Loughmore flourished under Esme's gentle influence. The townsfolk built a sanctuary dedicated to hope where they could gather in times of despair. This sanctuary was adorned with colorful ribbons, vibrant paintings, and cheerful flowers—symbols of the joy Esme had bestowed upon their lives. Esme's legacy extended far beyond Loughmora's boundaries. Her story spread like ripples in a vast lake, reaching those in need across the land. It wasn't long before neighboring towns sought Esme's presence, hoping to experience the magic that had transformed Loughmore. As she grew older, Esme never lost her ability to inspire. She nurtured new generations, teaching them about the power of kindness and the importance of fostering joy amidst whatever circumstances life presented. The world became her canvas, and she painted it with happiness, one smile at a time. Esme's journey came full circle as the years ticked by and she became an elder, her chestnut curls now dappled with gray. Loughmore had become a haven of love and laughter, a testament to what can be achieved through the resilience of the human spirit. When Esme finally closed her eyes for the very last time, the people of Loughmore did not mourn her passing. Instead, they celebrated a life well lived. They knew that, even without her physical presence, Esme's spirit would forever linger in the whispers of the wind and the warm rays of the summer sun. And so the legacy of the peculiar little girl named Esme lived on, her smile etched in the hearts of every soul touched by her joy. Loughmore continued to shine brightly, a beacon of hope and happiness, all thanks to the strange little girl who had transformed a town with a smile as potent as the summer sun. End`,
    playable: true,
  },
  {
    id: "the-lottery",
    title: "The Lottery",
    teaser: "A quiet village morning, an old tradition, and a truth that arrives too late.",
    body: `The morning of June 27th was clear and sunny, with the fresh warmth of a full- summer day; the flowers were blossoming profusely and the grass was richly green. The people of the village began to gather in the square, between the post office and the bank, around ten o'clock; in some towns there were so many people that the lottery took two days and had to be started on June 2th. but in this village, where there were only about three hundred people, the whole lottery took less than two hours, so it could begin at ten o'clock in the morning and still be through in time to allow the villagers to get home for noon dinner. The children assembled first, of course. School was recently over for the summer, and the feeling of liberty sat uneasily on most of them; they tended to gather together quietly for a while before they broke into boisterous play. and their talk was still of the classroom and the teacher, of books and reprimands. Bobby Martin had already stuffed his pockets full of stones, and the other boys soon followed his example, selecting the smoothest and roundest stones; Bobby and Harry Jones and Dickie Delacroix-- the villagers pronounced this name "Dellacroy"--eventually made a great pile of stones in one corner of the square and guarded it against the raids of the other boys. The girls stood aside, talking among themselves, looking over their shoulders at rolled in the dust or clung to the hands of their older brothers or sisters. Soon the men began to gather. surveying their own children, speaking of planting and rain, tractors and taxes. They stood together, away from the pile of stones in the corner, and their jokes were quiet and they smiled rather than laughed. The women, wearing faded house dresses and sweaters, came shortly after their menfolk. They greeted one another and exchanged bits of gossip as they went to join their husbands. Soon the women, standing by their husbands, began to call to their children, and the children came reluctantly, having to be called four or five times. Bobby Martin ducked under his mother's grasping hand and ran, laughing, back to the pile of stones. His father spoke up sharply, and Bobby came quickly and took his place between his father and his oldest brother. The lottery was conducted--as were the square dances, the teen club, the Halloween program--by Mr. Summers. who had time and energy to devote to civic activities. He was a round-faced, jovial man and he ran the coal business, and people were sorry for him. because he had no children and his wife was a scold. When he arrived in the square, carrying the black wooden box, there was a murmur of conversation among the villagers, and he waved and called. "Little late today, folks." The postmaster, Mr. Graves, followed him, carrying a three- legged stool, and the stool was put in the center of the square and Mr. Summers set the black box down on it. The villagers kept their distance, leaving a space between themselves and the stool. and when Mr. Summers said, "Some of you fellows want to give me a hand?" there was a hesitation before two men. Mr. Martin and his oldest son, Baxter. came forward to hold the box steady on the stool while Mr. Summers stirred up the papers inside it. The original paraphernalia for the lottery had been lost long ago, and the black box now resting on the stool had been put into use even before Old Man Warner, the oldest man in town, was born. Mr. Summers spoke frequently to the villagers about making a new box, but no one liked to upset even as much tradition as was represented by the black box. There was a story that the present box had been made with some pieces of the box that had preceded it, the one that had been constructed when the first people settled down to make a village here. Every year, after the lottery, Mr. Summers began talking again about a new box, but every year the subject was allowed to fade off without anything's being done. The black box grew shabbier each year: by now it was no longer completely black but splintered badly along one side to show the original wood color, and in some places faded or stained. Mr. Martin and his oldest son, Baxter, held the black box securely on the stool until Mr. Summers had stirred the papers thoroughly with his hand. Because so much of the ritual had been forgotten or discarded, Mr. Summers had been successful in having slips of paper substituted for the chips of wood that had been used for generations. Chips of wood, Mr. Summers had argued. had been all very well when the village was tiny, but now that the population was more than three hundred and likely to keep on growing, it was necessary to use something that would fit more easily into he black box. The night before the lottery, Mr. Summers and Mr. Graves made up the slips of paper and put them in the box, and it was then taken to the safe of Mr. Summers' coal company and locked up until Mr. Summers was ready to take it to the square next morning. The rest of the year, the box was put way, sometimes one place, sometimes another; it had spent one year in Mr. Graves's barn and another year underfoot in the post office. and sometimes it was set on a shelf in the Martin grocery and left there. There was a great deal of fussing to be done before Mr. Summers declared the lottery open. There were the lists to make up--of heads of families. heads of households in each family. members of each household in each family. There was the proper swearing-in of Mr. Summers by the postmaster, as the official of the lottery; at one time, some people remembered, there had been a recital of some sort, performed by the official of the lottery, a perfunctory. tuneless chant that had been rattled off duly each year; some people believed that the official of the lottery used to stand just so when he said or sang it, others believed that he was supposed to walk among the people, but years and years ago this p3rt of the ritual had been allowed to lapse. There had been, also, a ritual salute, which the official of the lottery had had to use in addressing each person who came up to draw from the box, but this also had changed with time, until now it was felt necessary only for the official to speak to each person approaching. Mr. Summers was very good at all this; in his clean white shirt and blue jeans. with one hand resting carelessly on the black box. he seemed very proper and important as he talked interminably to Mr. Graves and the Martins. Just as Mr. Summers finally left off talking and turned to the assembled villagers, Mrs. Hutchinson came hurriedly along the path to the square, her sweater thrown over her shoulders, and slid into place in the back of the crowd. "Clean forgot what day it was," she said to Mrs. Delacroix, who stood next to her, and they both laughed softly. "Thought my old man was out back stacking wood," Mrs. Hutchinson went on. "and then I looked out the window and the kids was gone, and then I remembered it was the twenty-seventh and came a-running." She dried her hands on her apron, and Mrs. Delacroix said, "You're in time, though. They're still talking away up there." Mrs. Hutchinson craned her neck to see through the crowd and found her husband and children standing near the front. She tapped Mrs. Delacroix on the arm as a farewell and began to make her way through the crowd. The people separated good-humoredly to let her through: two or three people said. in voices just loud enough to be heard across the crowd, "Here comes your, Missus, Hutchinson," and "Bill, she made it after all." Mrs. Hutchinson reached her husband, and Mr. Summers, who had been waiting, said cheerfully. "Thought we were going to have to get on without you, Tessie." Mrs. Hutchinson said. grinning, "Wouldn't have me leave m'dishes in the sink, now, would you. Joe?," and soft laughter ran through the crowd as the people stirred back into position after Mrs. Hutchinson's arrival. "Well, now." Mr. Summers said soberly, "guess we better get started, get this over with, so's we can go back to work. Anybody ain't here?" "Dunbar." several people said. "Dunbar. Dunbar." Mr. Summers consulted his list. "Clyde Dunbar." he said. "That's right. He's broke his leg, hasn't he? Who's drawing for him?" "Me. I guess," a woman said. and Mr. Summers turned to look at her. "Wife draws for her husband." Mr. Summers said. "Don't you have a grown boy to do it for you, Janey?" Although Mr. Summers and everyone else in the village knew the answer perfectly well, it was the business of the official of the lottery to ask such questions formally. Mr. Summers waited with an expression of polite interest while Mrs. Dunbar answered. "Horace's not but sixteen vet." Mrs. Dunbar said regretfully. "Guess I gotta fill in for the old man this year." "Right." Sr. Summers said. He made a note on the list he was holding. Then he asked, "Watson boy drawing this year?" A tall boy in the crowd raised his hand. "Here," he said. "I'm drawing for my mother and me." He blinked his eyes nervously and ducked his head as several voices in the crowd said thin#s like "Good fellow, lack." and "Glad to see your mother's got a man to do it." "Well," Mr. Summers said, "guess that's everyone. Old Man Warner make it?" "Here," a voice said. and Mr. Summers nodded. A sudden hush fell on the crowd as Mr. Summers cleared his throat and looked at the list. "All ready?" he called. "Now, I'll read the names--heads of families first--and the men come up and take a paper out of the box. Keep the paper folded in your hand without looking at it until everyone has had a turn. Everything clear?" The people had done it so many times that they only half listened to the directions: most of them were quiet. wetting their lips. not looking around. Then Mr. Summers raised one hand high and said, "Adams." A man disengaged himself from the crowd and came forward. "Hi. Steve." Mr. Summers said. and Mr. Adams said. "Hi. Joe." They grinned at one another humorlessly and nervously. Then Mr. Adams reached into the black box and took out a folded paper. He held it firmly by one corner as he turned and went hastily back to his place in the crowd. where he stood a little apart from his family. not looking down at his hand. "Allen." Mr. Summers said. "Anderson.... Bentham." "Seems like there's no time at all between lotteries any more." Mrs. Delacroix said to Mrs. Graves in the back row. "Seems like we got through with the last one only last week." "Time sure goes fast.-- Mrs. Graves said. "Clark.... Delacroix" "There goes my old man." Mrs. Delacroix said. She held her breath while her husband went forward. "Dunbar," Mr. Summers said, and Mrs. Dunbar went steadily to the box while one of the women said. "Go on. Janey," and another said, "There she goes." "We're next." Mrs. Graves said. She watched while Mr. Graves came around from the side of the box, greeted Mr. Summers gravely and selected a slip of paper from the box. By now, all through the crowd there were men holding the small folded papers in their large hand. turning them over and over nervously Mrs. Dunbar and her two sons stood together, Mrs. Dunbar holding the slip of paper. "Harburt.... Hutchinson." "Get up there, Bill," Mrs. Hutchinson said. and the people near her laughed. "Jones." "They do say," Mr. Adams said to Old Man Warner, who stood next to him, "that over in the north village they're talking of giving up the lottery." Old Man Warner snorted. "Pack of crazy fools," he said. "Listening to the young folks, nothing's good enough for them. Next thing you know, they'll be wanting to go back to living in caves, nobody work any more, live hat way for a while. Used to be a saying about 'Lottery in June, corn be heavy soon.' First thing you know, we'd all be eating stewed chickweed and acorns. There's always been a lottery," he added petulantly. "Bad enough to see young Joe Summers up there joking with everybody." "Some places have already quit lotteries." Mrs. Adams said. "Nothing but trouble in that," Old Man Warner said stoutly. "Pack of young fools." "Martin." And Bobby Martin watched his father go forward. "Overdyke.... Percy." "I wish they'd hurry," Mrs. Dunbar said to her older son. "I wish they'd hurry." "They're almost through," her son said. "You get ready to run tell Dad," Mrs. Dunbar said. Mr. Summers called his own name and then stepped forward precisely and selected a slip from the box. Then he called, "Warner." "Seventy-seventh year I been in the lottery," Old Man Warner said as he went through the crowd. "Seventy-seventh time." "Watson" The tall boy came awkwardly through the crowd. Someone said, "Don't be nervous, Jack," and Mr. Summers said, "Take your time, son." "Zanini." After that, there was a long pause, a breathless pause, until Mr. Summers. holding his slip of paper in the air, said, "All right, fellows." For a minute, no one moved, and then all the slips of paper were opened. Suddenly, all the women began to speak at once, saving. "Who is it?," "Who's got it?," "Is it the Dunbars?," "Is it the Watsons?" Then the voices began to say, "It's Hutchinson. It's Bill," "Bill Hutchinson's got it." "Go tell your father," Mrs. Dunbar said to her older son. People began to look around to see the Hutchinsons. Bill Hutchinson was standing quiet, staring down at the paper in his hand. Suddenly. Tessie Hutchinson shouted to Mr. Summers. "You didn't give him time enough to take any paper he wanted. I saw you. It wasn't fair!" "Be a good sport, Tessie." Mrs. Delacroix called, and Mrs. Graves said, "All of us took the same chance." "Shut up, Tessie," Bill Hutchinson said. "Well, everyone," Mr. Summers said, "that was done pretty fast, and now we've got to be hurrying a little more to get done in time." He consulted his next list. "Bill," he said, "you draw for the Hutchinson family. You got any other households in the Hutchinsons?" "There's Don and Eva," Mrs. Hutchinson yelled. "Make them take their chance!" "Daughters draw with their husbands' families, Tessie," Mr. Summers said gently. "You know that as well as anyone else." "It wasn't fair," Tessie said. "I guess not, Joe." Bill Hutchinson said regretfully. "My daughter draws with her husband's family; that's only fair. And I've got no other family except the kids." "Then, as far as drawing for families is concerned, it's you," Mr. Summers said in explanation, "and as far as drawing for households is concerned, that's you, too. Right?" "Right," Bill Hutchinson said. "How many kids, Bill?" Mr. Summers asked formally. "Three," Bill Hutchinson said. "There's Bill, Jr., and Nancy, and little Dave. And Tessie and me." "All right, then," Mr. Summers said. "Harry, you got their tickets back?" Mr. Graves nodded and held up the slips of paper. "Put them in the box, then," Mr. Summers directed. "Take Bill's and put it in." "I think we ought to start over," Mrs. Hutchinson said, as quietly as she could. "I tell you it wasn't fair. You didn't give him time enough to choose. Everybody saw that." Mr. Graves had selected the five slips and put them in the box. and he dropped all the papers but those onto the ground. where the breeze caught them and lifted them off. "Listen, everybody," Mrs. Hutchinson was saying to the people around her. "Ready, Bill?" Mr. Summers asked. and Bill Hutchinson, with one quick glance around at his wife and children. nodded. "Remember," Mr. Summers said. "take the slips and keep them folded until each person has taken one. Harry, you help little Dave." Mr. Graves took the hand of the little boy, who came willingly with him up to the box. "Take a paper out of the box, Davy." Mr. Summers said. Davy put his hand into the box and laughed. "Take just one paper." Mr. Summers said. "Harry, you hold it for him." Mr. Graves took the child's hand and removed the folded paper from the tight fist and held it while little Dave stood next to him and looked up at him wonderingly. "Nancy next," Mr. Summers said. Nancy was twelve, and her school friends breathed heavily as she went forward switching her skirt, and took a slip daintily from the box "Bill, Jr.," Mr. Summers said, and Billy, his face red and his feet overlarge, near knocked the box over as he got a paper out. "Tessie," Mr. Summers said. She hesitated for a minute, looking around defiantly. and then set her lips and went up to the box. She snatched a paper out and held it behind her. "Bill," Mr. Summers said, and Bill Hutchinson reached into the box and felt around, bringing his hand out at last with the slip of paper in it. The crowd was quiet. A girl whispered, "I hope it's not Nancy," and the sound of the whisper reached the edges of the crowd. "It's not the way it used to be." Old Man Warner said clearly. "People ain't the way they used to be." "All right," Mr. Summers said. "Open the papers. Harry, you open little Dave's." Mr. Graves opened the slip of paper and there was a general sigh through the crowd as he held it up and everyone could see that it was blank. Nancy and Bill. Jr.. opened theirs at the same time. and both beamed and laughed. turning around to the crowd and holding their slips of paper above their heads. "Tessie," Mr. Summers said. There was a pause, and then Mr. Summers looked at Bill Hutchinson, and Bill unfolded his paper and showed it. It was blank. "It's Tessie," Mr. Summers said, and his voice was hushed. "Show us her paper. Bill." Bill Hutchinson went over to his wife and forced the slip of paper out of her hand. It had a black spot on it, the black spot Mr. Summers had made the night before with the heavy pencil in the coal company office. Bill Hutchinson held it up, and there was a stir in the crowd. "All right, folks." Mr. Summers said. "Let's finish quickly." Although the villagers had forgotten the ritual and lost the original black box, they still remembered to use stones. The pile of stones the boys had made earlier was ready; there were stones on the ground with the blowing scraps of paper that had come out of the box Delacroix selected a stone so large she had to pick it up with both hands and turned to Mrs. Dunbar. "Come on," she said. "Hurry up." Mr. Dunbar had small stones in both hands, and she said. gasping for breath. "I can't run at all. You'll have to go ahead and I'll catch up with you." The children had stones already. And someone gave little Davy Hutchinson few pebbles. Tessie Hutchinson was in the center of a cleared space by now, and she held her hands out desperately as the villagers moved in on her. "It isn't fair," she said. A stone hit her on the side of the head. Old Man Warner was saying, "Come on, come on, everyone." Steve Adams was in the front of the crowd of villagers, with Mrs. Graves beside him. "It isn't fair, it isn't right," Mrs. Hutchinson screamed, and then they were upon her. End`,
    playable: true,
  },
  {
    id: "warmth-winter-storm",
    title: "Warmth in the Winter Storm",
    teaser: "A buried flashlight, a winter storm, and the small kindnesses that keep a family warm.",
    body: `Tommy's hand brushed against an old metal flashlight buried at the back of the storage closet. He frowned at first, half-expecting it to be one of those cheap plastic ones, but no, this one was solid and heavy. As he clicked the switch, the beam flickered to life, lighting up the corner of the garage. Suddenly, he was no longer standing in the present but in the winter of 1956. Tommy could almost smell the dusting of snow on the windows and hear the wind howling outside. He was 14 years old then, a teenager with big dreams of becoming a radio engineer. His fingers were often covered in grease and radio parts, tinkering with anything that hummed or buzzed. But that winter, something unexpected happened. A snowstorm hit, and it came on so quickly that before anyone could blink, they were all snowed in. No school, no work, just the four of them in a house that had suddenly started to feel a little too small. Tommy, irritated by the lack of space and constant interruptions to his radio experiments, sulked in his room. Rosie, who was equally upset about being cut off from her social life, sulked in hers. The entire house was grumpy, and the only noise was the howling wind outside. At the time, he and his sister Rosie didn't exactly get along. Rosie, at 12, was all about her friends and music, chatting on the phone for hours, while Tommy was more focused on building things, like his homemade radio—which, let's be honest, was mostly static and frustration. "Are you still messing with that radio?" Rosie asked, leaning against the doorframe, arms crossed. "It's called fixing stuff, Rosie. You should try it sometime." Tommy didn't even look up. "I'm not a nerd. I've got friends to talk to," she replied, her tone dripping with teenage attitude. "Maybe you should try it. Talking to people more." "People? Oh, like the same friends who you gossip with every day? Riveting stuff, really." Tommy rolled his eyes. "Better than listening to you drone on about static." Rosie smirked. As they continued to argue, the door creaked open, and their mother walked in, holding a laundry basket. "All right, you two. Knock it off," she said, her tone warm but firm. "The storm's getting worse, and you both have to survive each other." "But Mom," Rosie whined. "I have plans!" "Plans?" their mother asked, raising an eyebrow. "With the storm raging outside and no school for the next few days? I think your plans just changed." "I'm fine, Mom. I've got plenty to do. Don't worry about me." Tommy shrugged, still focused on the radio. But their mother wasn't having it. "I'll worry all I want, thank you very much. And what you two need is some family time. You're going to spend it together—no excuses." "Fine," Tommy muttered under his breath. He was about to go back to his tinkering when his mother reappeared, holding an old box of family photos. "Here," she said, setting it on the floor between them. "I found these in the attic. Let's go through them." "Photos? Really?" Tommy glanced at the box skeptically. "Why?" "Photos are boring, Mom." Rosie was less subtle. Their mother didn't say anything. She simply opened the box and pulled out a few pictures. "You'll thank me later," she said with a smile, taking away his radio. "Hey!" Tommy exclaimed. "I'm working here." "One hour family time. That's all I ask," their mother said as she left the living room. Reluctantly, they began looking through the pictures. The first picture threw Tommy. "Here, check this one out." He showed Rosie. It was a picture of himself, circa 1954, looking like a very confused four-year-old in a cowboy costume. His tiny hands were gripping the handle of a plastic gun, and his hat was clearly too big for him. Rosie burst into laughter, throwing her head back. "This is what you thought a cowboy looked like? Who's the real cowboy here?" Rosie grinned, showing Tommy a picture of their dad in a much more authentic-looking cowboy outfit from a family Halloween. Tommy stared at the picture of his younger self. "I mean, I was a cowboy. Obviously. Look at my confidence." "You were about 3 in. tall. Even the lollipop in your hand was bigger than your head," Rosie teased. Slowly, the atmosphere began to shift. Tommy picked up an old black-and-white photo of their grandparents on their wedding day. Rosie snorted but then stared at the picture longer than he expected. "This is actually kind of cute," she said, and Tommy laughed. "Look at Grandma. She looks like she's in a movie." "She was a movie star back then," Tommy joked. "Had a fan club and everything." "Uh… huh. That's funny, Tom," Rosie said, raising an eyebrow. Soon, the photos worked as their mother had hoped: They were talking—and laughing. The awkward tension faded as they dug through more pictures, sharing memories and stories from their childhood. As the night wore on, their parents joined in. They all ended up around the fireplace, pulling out old board games and telling funny stories. The storm raged on outside, but inside, they found warmth in each other's company. At some point, Rosie grabbed the flashlight sitting on the mantle. "Hey, remember when we used to do shadow puppet?" she said, grinning. "Let's do it!" The whole family gathered in front of the wall, the flashlight casting eerie shapes across the room. Rosie made her famous duck, and Tommy laughed as he tried to create a wolf that looked more like a squiggly mess. Their parents added to the chaos, making shadows of everything from a cat to a flying saucer. Laughter filled the room, loud and genuine. The snowstorm that had felt so confining had turned into one of the best memories of Tommy's life. Standing in the storage closet, holding the flashlight, he felt a deep gratitude—for Rosie, his family, and the warmth they'd found that cold winter night. It wasn't just the storm that brought them together; it was the laughter, the memories, and the little moments of joy. "Thanks, Mom," Tommy whispered, placing the flashlight back on the shelf. End`,
    playable: true,
  },
  {
    id: "families-behind-summit",
    title: "The Families Behind the Summit",
    teaser: "Everest, 1953 — and the quiet courage of the families who waited below.",
    body: `The year was 1953, and Mount Everest was about to meet its match in the form of Edmund Hillary and Tenzing Norgay. The climb was historic, the stakes enormous, and on the other side of the world, two families were holding their breath. In New Zealand, Edmund's family was practically living in the living room, gathered around the radio, hoping for any shred of information. The tension in their home was palpable, as if the entire house had been transformed into a pressure cooker, ready to explode with every passing hour. Margaret Hillary, Edmund's mother, was the embodiment of a proud mother holding it together. She smiled at her children, but anyone could tell that the smile didn't quite reach her eyes. Every day, she'd give her best attempt at being upbeat, but she couldn't stop the worry gnawing at her heart. Each minute felt like a year. "You know, he always had that fire in him," she'd say to her younger children, trying to reassure them. "He's made of tough stuff, just like his father." Her younger son, George, would nod enthusiastically, trying to be optimistic. "Of course, Mum! He'll be fine. He's got the mountain in his sights!" he said. But even George's cheerful words didn't entirely wipe the anxiety that loomed in the air. They'd hear updates from time to time, small bits of news that either sparked hope or left them worrying even more. Each telegram felt like it weighed a thousand pounds, but there was always a delay. A day, sometimes two, with no word at all. Over in Nepal, across the globe, Tenzing Norgay's family was doing what they could to hold onto hope as well. His wife, Ang Lhamu, stood out in the cool morning air, looking at the distant mountains. She had faith, sure, but every time she looked up, her heart clenched. Tenzing had been away for weeks. "May the mountain be kind to him," she whispered under her breath. "May it let him come home." For both families, it wasn't just the silence that felt heavy; it was the waiting. What do you do when you're waiting for life-changing news, and the universe won't tell you anything? How do you fill that time? Back in New Zealand, Margaret tried to distract herself by sorting through family photos. She'd sit at the kitchen table, holding each photo as if it could somehow tell her what was going on with Edmund. And then, one day, there was a tiny glimmer of hope, a message that arrived with a faint optimism: The team is making progress. They've reached higher altitudes, and we're hopeful. It wasn't much, but it was something. "See, I told you!" George grinned, running to tell his siblings. "He's climbing, Mum. He's climbing higher than anyone thought possible!" Margaret let herself feel the relief seep in, even if just for a second. Her son was still alive. She wiped her eyes and smiled. "Yes, yes. He's always been determined, hasn't he?" she said. On the other side of the world, in Nepal, Ang Lhamu was lighting another small lamp as she heard similar news: Progress being made. We hope for a successful summit. A smile cracked across her face. She couldn't say it out loud—no, that would jinx it—but in her heart, she felt a flicker of belief. Maybe, just maybe, Tenzing was close to achieving his dream. However, like all good things, it wasn't just a smooth ride from there. Days stretched on without communication, and the silence grew more unbearable by the minute. The past few messages had been vague, and the families grew increasingly anxious. How could a mountain be so silent? Why weren't they hearing anything more specific? Each day without a word felt like an eternity. The worst news came a few days later. The previous attempt to reach the summit had failed, the other expedition team hadn't made it, and there was no new word about Tenzing or Edmund. That was when the sleepless nights started. Margaret was up late, pacing the floor. Every sound from the radio, every shuffle of paper, made her jump. In Nepal, Ang Lhamu's prayers became more desperate, more urgent. Her rituals increased, but the uncertainty clawed at her, shaking her faith. Weeks passed by, but the silence only stretched on. And then—finally—the message came. The telegram arrived in New Zealand first, the thick paper almost trembling in Margaret's hands as she read the first few lines. The words "Edmund Hillary and Tenzing Norgay" jumped off the page like they were made of light. Margaret read the message twice before it sank in. "Edmund… he did it. He… he did it!" Margaret gasped, her voice cracking as the realization washed over her. In Nepal, Ang Lhamu was at the door of their home when a messenger arrived with the same news. Tenzing's family gathered around, their faces incredulous as the words sank in. "They made it. They've reached the summit!" Ang Lhamu could hardly contain herself. She let out a joyful cry and ran to her children, wrapping them in her arms. Tears flowed freely now, no longer held back. Her husband, the man she had watched work tirelessly for this moment, had done it. Both families had their moment of overwhelming emotion. For Edmund's family, it was relief mingled with pride, and for Tenzing's, it was an immense sense of accomplishment. They hadn't just witnessed the summit of a mountain; they had witnessed the culmination of years of hard work, hope, and unyielding love. Later, as Margaret read her telegram aloud to the family, she remembered her son's determination. "From the time he was young, he knew exactly what he wanted," she said, looking at her younger children. "And when he sets his mind to something… well, there's no stopping him." She smiled a soft but proud smile, knowing that Edmund's success would inspire generations to come. End`,
    playable: true,
  },
  {
    id: "taste-of-freedom",
    title: "The Taste of Freedom",
    teaser: "A ripe peach, a spring day in 1954, and the memory of a life finally free.",
    body: `It was a warm spring day in 1954 when Jack's three-year-old grandchild, little Timmy, took a big, juicy bite of a ripe peach for the first time, his face lighting up with delight. Jack watched with a smile, remembering a moment from his own childhood when the taste of fresh fruit felt just as magical. Jack's eyes twinkled as he set his cup of tea down. "Ah, Timmy," Jack muttered softly, "if you only knew the taste of a fruit after years of having to count every single ounce of sugar." He chuckled to himself, realizing that the little boy probably couldn't imagine a time when he couldn't just pick a fruit off a tree or grab a snack at the store. It all started with the end of food rationing, which had plagued the country for so long. The day it officially ended, Jack and his sister, Anna, had been filled with curiosity and excitement. In the postwar years, they had only known empty shelves, long queues, and a menu that often consisted of bare and bland meals. But this was different—this was a celebration, a grand reintroduction to the pleasures of life that had been so sorely missed. Jack still remembered that day as if it were yesterday. He and Anna had spent the whole week listening to the radio, waiting for that moment when rationing would finally be over. Jack's mother had spent days planning and trying to find some extra money to take them out to the market for the first time in years. She finally announced, with a gleam in her eye, "We're going to the market today, and it's going to be a treat." When they arrived at the market, the sight took his breath away. Everywhere, there were stalls overflowing with fruits and vegetables—things he hadn't seen in what seemed like a lifetime. There were oranges that looked like they had just been picked from a sunlit orchard, shiny red apples, and ripe bananas. There was chocolate, not the small, rationed squares, but big, glossy bars. And bread—oh, the bread—so soft, so pillowy, the kind you could almost smell from a mile away. Anna was practically bouncing with excitement, her eyes as wide as saucers. She pointed to the oranges first. "Can we get one, Mum?" she asked, barely able to contain herself. "Of course," their mother said, laughing. "It's a special day." Anna, grinning from ear to ear, was already halfway through her first orange. "Mum, this is like a dream!" she said, juice dribbling down her chin. Their mother filled their basket with things they hadn't had in ages—meats, vegetables, and the most wonderful thing of all: fresh milk. "We'll have a feast tonight," she said, her voice full of warmth. As they made their way home, Jack couldn't help but feel a new sense of gratitude. He had always been a bit of a dreamer, thinking about gadgets and inventions, but that day, as he sat down to dinner with his family, his mind was entirely taken by the food. The smell of roast chicken, buttered potatoes, and vegetables flooded the house. The air was thick with the kind of comfort only a hearty meal could provide. Sitting at the table that night, Jack looked around. His father was carving the chicken, and Anna was talking excitedly about all the fruits she had to eat. As the evening went on, the conversation turned to stories of their past. His mother shared her memories of the Great Depression, and his father told them about the reason for the food shortages during the war. Both Anna and Jack listened, wide-eyed. "You kids don't know how lucky you are," their father said, leaning back in his chair with a contented sigh. Jack didn't quite understand it at the time, but as he took his first bite of roast chicken, his mouth exploded with so many juicy flavors. "Wow!" he exclaimed. "This is delicious! Why haven't we always had this kind of meal?" His parents shared a knowing look. Anna, her mouth full of potatoes, giggled but then quieted when she noticed their mother's smile fade into something softer and more serious. "Oh, Jackie," his mother said gently. "You see, during the war and for years after, there wasn't always enough to go around. We had ration books—little coupons that told us how much sugar or meat we could have each week. And that wasn't very much at all." Jack's father nodded, setting down the carving knife. "Think about your fruit tart there," he said, pointing to the sweet pastry on Jack's plate. "You know, before today, we might've only been allowed a few ounces of sugar per week. Enough for a couple of cups of tea, maybe, but certainly not enough to make a tart like this." "And these potatoes," his mother chimed in with a fond laugh. "And that's true for lots of other things—like meat, butter, eggs. You name it, and we likely had to use a coupon for it." Jack paused, glancing from the chicken platter to the heaping bowl of potatoes. "But it's going to be different now," Anna, fiddling with her fork, chimed in. "We are no longer gonna see empty shelves in the shops. Mum won't have to make us soup out of scraps or reuse old recipes from leftover vegetables." "Is it all really gonna change?" Jack asked hopefully. "No more watery stews and bland porridge?" "Yes, honey, those days are behind us now." His mother reached across the table and squeezed Jack's hand. "We're just happy that you and Anna can grow up in a world where you can enjoy all these things. This roast chicken, these potatoes, the fruit tarts—it's not something to take for granted." Jack glanced around, taking in the scene. His father was beaming with quiet pride, his mother looked relieved in a way he hadn't noticed before, and Anna was happily polishing off a second helping of potatoes. It all felt so wonderfully normal, yet he could sense a lingering depth in his parents' words—something they'd fought hard to reach. The memory made Jack chuckle softly, looking over at Timmy. "Ah, Timmy, if only you knew what it was like before all this," he said with a wink. Timmy just smiled, juice dripping down his chin, utterly unaware of the powerful history of that simple, delicious bite. But to Jack, it was more than just a piece of fruit; it was the taste of freedom. End`,
    playable: true,
  },
  {
    id: "gift-of-the-magi",
    title: "The Gift of the Magi",
    teaser: "One dollar and eighty-seven cents — and the wisest gift two hearts can give.",
    body: `One dollar and eighty-seven cents. That was all. And sixty cents of it was in pennies. Pennies saved one and two at a time by bulldozing the grocer and the vegetable man and the butcher until one's cheeks burned with the silent imputation of parsimony that such close dealing implied. Three times Della counted it. One dollar and eighty- seven cents. And the next day would be Christmas. There was clearly nothing to do but flop down on the shabby little couch and howl. So Della did it. Which instigates the moral reflection that life is made up of sobs, sniffles, and smiles, with sniffles predominating. While the mistress of the home is gradually subsiding from the first stage to the second, take a look at the home. A furnished flat at $8 per week. It did not exactly beggar description, but it certainly had that word on the lookout for the mendicancy squad. In the vestibule below was a letter-box into which no letter would go, and an electric button from which no mortal finger could coax a ring. Also appertaining thereunto was a card bearing the name "Mr. James Dillingham Young." The "Dillingham" had been flung to the breeze during a former period of prosperity when its possessor was being paid $30 per week. Now, when the income was shrunk to $20, though, they were thinking seriously of contracting to a modest and unassuming D. But whenever Mr. James Dillingham Young came home and reached his flat above he was called "Jim" and greatly hugged by Mrs. James Dillingham Young, already introduced to you as Della. Which is all very good. Della finished her cry and attended to her cheeks with the powder rag. She stood by the window and looked out dully at a gray cat walking a gray fence in a gray backyard. Tomorrow would be Christmas Day, and she had only $1.87 with which to buy Jim a present. She had been saving every penny she could for months, with this result. Twenty dollars a week doesn't go far. Expenses had been greater than she had calculated. They always are. Only $1.87 to buy a present for Jim. Her Jim. Many a happy hour she had spent planning for something nice for him. Something fine and rare and sterling-- something just a little bit near to being worthy of the honor of being owned by Jim. There was a pier-glass between the windows of the room. Perhaps you have seen a pier-glass in an $8 flat. A very thin and very agile person may, by observing his reflection in a rapid sequence of longitudinal strips, obtain a fairly accurate conception of his looks. Della, being slender, had mastered the art. Suddenly she whirled from the window and stood before the glass. her eyes were shining brilliantly, but her face had lost its color within twenty seconds. Rapidly she pulled down her hair and let it fall to its full length. Now, there were two possessions of the James Dillingham Youngs in which they both took a mighty pride. One was Jim's gold watch that had been his father's and his grandfather's. The other was Della's hair. Had the queen of Sheba lived in the flat across the airshaft, Della would have let her hair hang out the window some day to dry just to depreciate Her Majesty's jewels and gifts. Had King Solomon been the janitor, with all his treasures piled up in the basement, Jim would have pulled out his watch every time he passed, just to see him pluck at his beard from envy. So now Della's beautiful hair fell about her rippling and shining like a cascade of brown waters. It reached below her knee and made itself almost a garment for her. And then she did it up again nervously and quickly. Once she faltered for a minute and stood still while a tear or two splashed on the worn red carpet. On went her old brown jacket; on went her old brown hat. With a whirl of skirts and with the brilliant sparkle still in her eyes, she fluttered out the door and down the stairs to the street. Where she stopped the sign read: "Mne. Sofronie. Hair Goods of All Kinds." One flight up Della ran, and collected herself, panting. Madame, large, too white, chilly, hardly looked the "Sofronie." "Will you buy my hair?" asked Della. "I buy hair," said Madame. "Take yer hat off and let's have a sight at the looks of it." Down rippled the brown cascade. "Twenty dollars," said Madame, lifting the mass with a practised hand. "Give it to me quick," said Della. Oh, and the next two hours tripped by on rosy wings. Forget the hashed metaphor. She was ransacking the stores for Jim's present. She found it at last. It surely had been made for Jim and no one else. There was no other like it in any of the stores, and she had turned all of them inside out. It was a platinum fob chain simple and chaste in design, properly proclaiming its value by substance alone and not by meretricious ornamentation--as all good things should do. It was even worthy of The Watch. As soon as she saw it she knew that it must be Jim's. It was like him. Quietness and value--the description applied to both. Twenty-one dollars they took from her for it, and she hurried home with the 87 cents. With that chain on his watch Jim might be properly anxious about the time in any company. Grand as the watch was, he sometimes looked at it on the sly on account of the old leather strap that he used in place of a chain. When Della reached home her intoxication gave way a little to prudence and reason. She got out her curling irons and lighted the gas and went to work repairing the ravages made by generosity added to love. Which is always a tremendous task, dear friends--a mammoth task. Within forty minutes her head was covered with tiny, close-lying curls that made her look wonderfully like a truant schoolboy. She looked at her reflection in the mirror long, carefully, and critically. "If Jim doesn't kill me," she said to herself, "before he takes a second look at me, he'll say I look like a Coney Island chorus girl. But what could I do--oh! what could I do with a dollar and eighty- seven cents?" At 7 o'clock the coffee was made and the frying-pan was on the back of the stove hot and ready to cook the chops. Jim was never late. Della doubled the fob chain in her hand and sat on the corner of the table near the door that he always entered. Then she heard his step on the stair away down on the first flight, and she turned white for just a moment. She had a habit for saying little silent prayer about the simplest everyday things, and now she whispered: "Please God, make him think I am still pretty." The door opened and Jim stepped in and closed it. He looked thin and very serious. Poor fellow, he was only twenty-two--and to be burdened with a family! He needed a new overcoat and he was without gloves. Jim stopped inside the door, as immovable as a setter at the scent of quail. His eyes were fixed upon Della, and there was an expression in them that she could not read, and it terrified her. It was not anger, nor surprise, nor disapproval, nor horror, nor any of the sentiments that she had been prepared for. He simply stared at her fixedly with that peculiar expression on his face. Della wriggled off the table and went for him. "Jim, darling," she cried, "don't look at me that way. I had my hair cut off and sold because I couldn't have lived through Christmas without giving you a present. It'll grow out again--you won't mind, will you? I just had to do it. My hair grows awfully fast. Say \`Merry Christmas!' Jim, and let's be happy. You don't know what a nice-- what a beautiful, nice gift I've got for you." "You've cut off your hair?" asked Jim, laboriously, as if he had not arrived at that patent fact yet even after the hardest mental labor. "Cut it off and sold it," said Della. "Don't you like me just as well, anyhow? I'm me without my hair, ain't I?" Jim looked about the room curiously. "You say your hair is gone?" he said, with an air almost of idiocy. "You needn't look for it," said Della. "It's sold, I tell you--sold and gone, too. It's Christmas Eve, boy. Be good to me, for it went for you. Maybe the hairs of my head were numbered," she went on with sudden serious sweetness, "but nobody could ever count my love for you. Shall I put the chops on, Jim?" Out of his trance Jim seemed quickly to wake. He enfolded his Della. For ten seconds let us regard with discreet scrutiny some inconsequential object in the other direction. Eight dollars a week or a million a year--what is the difference? A mathematician or a wit would give you the wrong answer. The magi brought valuable gifts, but that was not among them. This dark assertion will be illuminated later on. Jim drew a package from his overcoat pocket and threw it upon the table. "Don't make any mistake, Dell," he said, "about me. I don't think there's anything in the way of a haircut or a shave or a shampoo that could make me like my girl any less. But if you'll unwrap that package you may see why you had me going a while at first." White fingers and nimble tore at the string and paper. And then an ecstatic scream of joy; and then, alas! a quick feminine change to hysterical tears and wails, necessitating the immediate employment of all the comforting powers of the lord of the flat. For there lay The Combs--the set of combs, side and back, that Della had worshipped long in a Broadway window. Beautiful combs, pure tortoise shell, with jewelled rims-- just the shade to wear in the beautiful vanished hair. They were expensive combs, she knew, and her heart had simply craved and yearned over them without the least hope of possession. And now, they were hers, but the tresses that should have adorned the coveted adornments were gone. But she hugged them to her bosom, and at length she was able to look up with dim eyes and a smile and say: "My hair grows so fast, Jim!" And them Della leaped up like a little singed cat and cried, "Oh, oh!" Jim had not yet seen his beautiful present. She held it out to him eagerly upon her open palm. The dull precious metal seemed to flash with a reflection of her bright and ardent spirit. "Isn't it a dandy, Jim? I hunted all over town to find it. You'll have to look at the time a hundred times a day now. Give me your watch. I want to see how it looks on it." Instead of obeying, Jim tumbled down on the couch and put his hands under the back of his head and smiled. "Dell," said he, "let's put our Christmas presents away and keep 'em a while. They're too nice to use just at present. I sold the watch to get the money to buy your combs. And now suppose you put the chops on." The magi, as you know, were wise men--wonderfully wise men--who brought gifts to the Babe in the manger. They invented the art of giving Christmas presents. Being wise, their gifts were no doubt wise ones, possibly bearing the privilege of exchange in case of duplication. And here I have lamely related to you the uneventful chronicle of two foolish children in a flat who most unwisely sacrificed for each other the greatest treasures of their house. But in a last word to the wise of these days let it be said that of all who give gifts these two were the wisest. O all who give and receive gifts, such as they are wisest. Everywhere they are wisest. They are the magi. End`,
    playable: true,
  }
];

export function getStory(id: string): Story | null {
  return STORIES.find((s) => s.id === id) ?? null;
}
